1
00:00:04,334 --> 00:00:07,703
(Day Four, evening)

2
00:00:09,706 --> 00:00:12,607
Let me explain the theory...

3
00:00:12,608 --> 00:00:15,310
I mentioned earlier.

4
00:00:15,612 --> 00:00:17,413
There's a spy,

5
00:00:18,382 --> 00:00:21,750
and the Reporter gets
to make a choice, right?

6
00:00:22,827 --> 00:00:24,094
- Should we continue outside?
- Yes.

7
00:00:32,128 --> 00:00:34,396
The spy is someone whose role is...

8
00:00:34,397 --> 00:00:36,264
to create division among the residents.

9
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:41,737
The spy's only goal is to make sure that,
in the final game on the last day,

10
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:46,308
the residents paired up in teams of two
are unable to trust one another.

11
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:48,243
If the residents fail
the final trust game...

12
00:00:48,244 --> 00:00:51,713
because they cannot trust each other,

13
00:00:52,382 --> 00:00:55,284
the spy will take all the prize money...

14
00:00:55,285 --> 00:00:57,152
they have accumulated up to that point.

15
00:00:57,687 --> 00:00:59,755
That means the more residents...

16
00:00:59,756 --> 00:01:02,958
who survive until the end
and the more prize money they accumulate,

17
00:01:02,959 --> 00:01:06,029
the larger the amount
the spy can ultimately win.

18
00:01:06,596 --> 00:01:11,166
To help accomplish this, the spy is
given the following privileges.

19
00:01:11,634 --> 00:01:14,202
First, once a day,

20
00:01:14,203 --> 00:01:17,172
the spy may view the code scores
of any resident they choose.

21
00:01:17,173 --> 00:01:18,239
Nangja.

22
00:01:18,341 --> 00:01:20,776
(Residents whose codes
have been viewed so far: Nangja)

23
00:01:20,777 --> 00:01:23,045
(Michael)

24
00:01:23,046 --> 00:01:25,047
Second, every morning,

25
00:01:25,048 --> 00:01:28,150
the spy may check all residents'
current personal fund balances.

26
00:01:28,551 --> 00:01:30,418
She won. Go Ae Shin won.

27
00:01:41,264 --> 00:01:44,533
Using these two pieces of information,
codes and money,

28
00:01:44,534 --> 00:01:48,003
the spy will work out who is
likely to clash with whom...

29
00:01:48,004 --> 00:01:51,974
and which residents are least likely
to trust each other.

30
00:01:52,308 --> 00:01:54,075
If you sit in the corner,
they can't see you.

31
00:01:58,430 --> 00:02:01,731
- You must feel pretty uneasy, right?
- What is this about?

32
00:02:02,519 --> 00:02:06,789
The Reporter seems
like a really powerful role, doesn't it?

33
00:02:07,724 --> 00:02:12,361
If I can give the Reporter
a little information she should know...

34
00:02:13,596 --> 00:02:14,596
Let's say...

35
00:02:15,231 --> 00:02:17,098
the Reporter has 100 dollars.

36
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:22,304
Her chat ID starts with "tot."

37
00:02:23,039 --> 00:02:23,839
And then,

38
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:29,210
there's "REWC."

39
00:02:32,525 --> 00:02:35,360
He knew all four
of my ideology code scores exactly.

40
00:02:35,385 --> 00:02:37,620
He also knew exactly
how much money I had left.

41
00:02:37,821 --> 00:02:41,056
"How could he know it all so precisely?"
He got everything exactly right.

42
00:02:41,057 --> 00:02:43,459
So that's when I thought,
"Oh, it must be him."

43
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:45,461
"He is the spy."

44
00:02:45,462 --> 00:02:48,931
From the Reporter's point of view,
she can decide after seeing all the cards.

45
00:02:48,932 --> 00:02:51,634
"I know your code. I know your ID."

46
00:02:51,635 --> 00:02:53,469
"And I know
you only have 100 dollars left."

47
00:02:53,470 --> 00:02:55,338
At that point,
the Reporter has to choose, right?

48
00:02:55,339 --> 00:02:57,372
She could run and ask them to save her.

49
00:02:57,373 --> 00:02:59,842
But that's when I need to tell her,
"You don't have to."

50
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:04,346
The spy probably already knows
a lot of things anyway,

51
00:03:04,347 --> 00:03:06,715
so that kind of information
may not actually matter that much.

52
00:03:06,716 --> 00:03:10,152
I don't think eliminating people is...

53
00:03:10,153 --> 00:03:11,887
necessarily the spy's main goal.

54
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:16,324
In fact, it might actually be better
for the spy if everyone stays happy...

55
00:03:16,592 --> 00:03:18,193
and survives for as long as possible.

56
00:03:18,194 --> 00:03:21,029
Doesn't that make him almost all-powerful?

57
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:23,765
At the end of the spy's journey,

58
00:03:23,766 --> 00:03:26,735
they may ultimately choose
to betray everyone, or they may not.

59
00:03:27,303 --> 00:03:30,139
But to reach the point
where they have that kind of power,

60
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:31,873
the spy has to wait.

61
00:03:31,874 --> 00:03:34,209
That's why the spy needs the Reporter.

62
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:36,478
The spy also holds
the power of life and death,

63
00:03:36,813 --> 00:03:37,913
so it's a very strange relationship.

64
00:03:38,781 --> 00:03:41,015
The Reporter has access
to a lot of information,

65
00:03:41,016 --> 00:03:43,852
but if I were the Reporter, there are
two things I'd want to find out.

66
00:03:44,363 --> 00:03:47,665
First, is there a way
for both the Reporter and the spy...

67
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:50,925
to survive until the end
and make money together?

68
00:03:51,294 --> 00:03:52,461
Is that actually possible?

69
00:03:52,838 --> 00:03:55,373
Or second, if the spy is eliminated,

70
00:03:55,398 --> 00:03:56,998
does the spy role disappear completely,

71
00:03:57,433 --> 00:04:00,335
or does someone else become the new spy?

72
00:04:00,336 --> 00:04:02,470
I think finding out those two things...

73
00:04:02,805 --> 00:04:04,572
would be most beneficial to the Reporter.

74
00:04:05,408 --> 00:04:09,244
And the spy would never lie
to you about that.

75
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:12,180
"Find out for yourself
whether there's a way..."

76
00:04:12,181 --> 00:04:15,651
"for you and me to survive together
until the very end."

77
00:04:15,652 --> 00:04:17,219
That's all I need to tell her now.

78
00:04:17,988 --> 00:04:19,088
Do you know...

79
00:04:19,989 --> 00:04:22,891
what the odds are
of someone just guessing yours correctly?

80
00:04:23,593 --> 00:04:24,826
One in 64.

81
00:04:25,962 --> 00:04:26,895
They can't do it.

82
00:04:27,165 --> 00:04:29,798
That's why someone with the right profile
became the Reporter.

83
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:33,501
Your profile itself has
only a one in 64 chance...

84
00:04:33,502 --> 00:04:35,537
of being guessed correctly at random.

85
00:04:35,538 --> 00:04:37,705
No one can eliminate you.

86
00:04:37,706 --> 00:04:38,940
And I'm sure she knows...

87
00:04:38,941 --> 00:04:41,110
that her profile is pretty unusual too.

88
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:43,111
She's a woman,
but she leans toward equalism...

89
00:04:43,112 --> 00:04:44,212
by two points.

90
00:04:44,213 --> 00:04:46,014
The other categories are
even harder to predict.

91
00:04:46,015 --> 00:04:49,284
But I can eliminate her because I know.
One shot is all I need.

92
00:04:49,285 --> 00:04:52,453
Then again, I also have
immunity from elimination.

93
00:04:52,881 --> 00:04:54,681
"You're going to try to take me out?"

94
00:04:54,758 --> 00:04:56,725
"Then you're going down too." That's it.

95
00:04:57,594 --> 00:05:01,263
I don't know if this will help
you enjoy the game more.

96
00:05:01,397 --> 00:05:04,999
It helped a lot.
That was a very good answer.

97
00:05:05,134 --> 00:05:07,235
I think it's risky for us...

98
00:05:07,236 --> 00:05:10,038
to talk alone for this long.
People are watching us.

99
00:05:10,039 --> 00:05:11,673
Benjamin can barely speak Korean,

100
00:05:12,375 --> 00:05:14,376
so if he's having such a long conversation
with the Reporter,

101
00:05:15,111 --> 00:05:16,211
won't they start wondering?

102
00:05:16,779 --> 00:05:20,415
I brought this up yesterday.

103
00:05:20,583 --> 00:05:23,818
I said that maybe the Reporter could work
for the residents,

104
00:05:23,819 --> 00:05:25,987
or maybe the Reporter could work
for the spy.

105
00:05:25,988 --> 00:05:27,889
It was just a theory of mine.

106
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:30,025
That's what we've been talking
about just now.

107
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:34,596
And since I didn't get a clear answer...

108
00:05:35,198 --> 00:05:37,833
and was simply told to wait and see,

109
00:05:38,101 --> 00:05:40,469
I'm going to go tell
the other residents about it now.

110
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:42,471
- It's an honor.
- It's an honor for me too.

111
00:05:44,407 --> 00:05:45,707
(Tensions Under the Roof)

112
00:05:45,842 --> 00:05:48,177
(The Community)

113
00:05:49,397 --> 00:05:50,812
I don't want to wash up yet.

114
00:05:51,547 --> 00:05:53,147
Maybe I should change too.

115
00:05:53,148 --> 00:05:55,185
- I'm all wet.
- Shall we?

116
00:05:55,186 --> 00:05:57,286
- Right.
- Let's meet again after we freshen up.

117
00:05:57,287 --> 00:05:58,119
What is it now?

118
00:06:00,523 --> 00:06:01,790
Another anonymous chat?

119
00:06:02,125 --> 00:06:03,792
From now until midnight,

120
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:06,962
registration for Lifetime Leader
candidates will be open.

121
00:06:08,097 --> 00:06:11,100
Residents should visit
the Community Center...

122
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:13,859
- and declare their support.
- Yes.

123
00:06:14,070 --> 00:06:16,538
Once a candidate receives
support from four residents,

124
00:06:16,964 --> 00:06:21,067
their registration as a Lifetime Leader
candidate will be complete.

125
00:06:21,611 --> 00:06:22,911
We'd better be really smart about this.

126
00:06:22,912 --> 00:06:25,714
Please note that once you declare
your support,

127
00:06:25,715 --> 00:06:27,516
it cannot be withdrawn or changed.

128
00:06:27,517 --> 00:06:29,451
Should we just jump in
and declare our support?

129
00:06:29,452 --> 00:06:31,420
But we haven't talked about it yet.

130
00:06:31,421 --> 00:06:34,389
This is definitely going to snowball
into something huge.

131
00:06:34,825 --> 00:06:37,593
- This is going to be a huge variable!
- Right.

132
00:06:37,826 --> 00:06:39,494
This is so scary.

133
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:42,230
Registration for Lifetime Leader
opens soon.

134
00:06:42,231 --> 00:06:43,598
I haven't thought about it.

135
00:06:44,666 --> 00:06:46,801
Well, we've already made up our minds.

136
00:06:47,102 --> 00:06:49,738
Did you see this? Registration
for Lifetime Leader candidates.

137
00:06:50,039 --> 00:06:51,306
They need people's support.

138
00:06:51,789 --> 00:06:53,775
I want us to wait until the very end
to declare our support.

139
00:06:53,776 --> 00:06:56,211
The people who already promised me
should go first.

140
00:06:56,546 --> 00:06:58,195
(Recruitment targets
of the four-person alliance: Dark Knight,)

141
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:00,315
(Benjamin,)

142
00:07:00,316 --> 00:07:02,050
(Genie)

143
00:07:02,284 --> 00:07:04,586
- Okay.
- Got it. Let's go for now.

144
00:07:04,587 --> 00:07:05,887
Let's go for now.

145
00:07:07,123 --> 00:07:09,624
Let's cook dinner first.

146
00:07:10,093 --> 00:07:11,927
I think we need Superman.

147
00:07:11,928 --> 00:07:13,529
Really? Then I'll go get him.

148
00:07:17,333 --> 00:07:18,967
They want you in the kitchen, Chef.

149
00:07:19,869 --> 00:07:21,136
Did you talk to them?

150
00:07:21,838 --> 00:07:23,972
Well, yes. Benjamin said yes.

151
00:07:24,841 --> 00:07:27,142
And I talked with Genie for a long time,

152
00:07:27,143 --> 00:07:28,377
so I just have to talk to her a bit more.

153
00:07:28,378 --> 00:07:30,446
I think Genie will say yes.

154
00:07:30,447 --> 00:07:32,381
So there are the four of us,
plus Genie and Benjamin.

155
00:07:32,382 --> 00:07:33,982
Those are the votes we've secured so far.

156
00:07:33,983 --> 00:07:35,684
Gray said he had a serious conversation
with Ted.

157
00:07:35,685 --> 00:07:37,052
Really? I don't know about that.

158
00:07:37,253 --> 00:07:40,155
As for the election,

159
00:07:40,356 --> 00:07:43,024
we need seven votes to win anyway.

160
00:07:43,025 --> 00:07:47,362
But Benjamin and I were talking earlier...

161
00:07:47,363 --> 00:07:50,532
about trying to keep as many people...

162
00:07:50,533 --> 00:07:52,834
from being eliminated as possible.

163
00:07:52,835 --> 00:07:56,304
So I wanted to ask what you think...

164
00:07:56,305 --> 00:07:58,573
about using certain safeguards
to help people survive.

165
00:07:59,242 --> 00:08:01,810
I'm only just starting
to think about that idea myself...

166
00:08:02,145 --> 00:08:04,079
If he ever tried...

167
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:05,814
to wield absolute power,

168
00:08:05,815 --> 00:08:07,716
then of course I'd join you.
But if that's not the case,

169
00:08:08,184 --> 00:08:11,820
I don't think I should rush into anything.

170
00:08:11,821 --> 00:08:14,322
Of course, I'm not forcing you to join us.

171
00:08:14,824 --> 00:08:17,325
It isn't easy to form a close bond
with Ted.

172
00:08:17,326 --> 00:08:21,296
He's very rational and tends
to judge things based on the situation.

173
00:08:21,297 --> 00:08:24,400
He's the type who only makes a decision
once the circumstances...

174
00:08:24,425 --> 00:08:26,492
actually call for it,
so he's very cautious.

175
00:08:27,236 --> 00:08:29,304
- Okay. Sir...
- What's the problem?

176
00:08:29,305 --> 00:08:30,872
- The chicken!
- It's for the boiled chicken soup.

177
00:08:30,873 --> 00:08:32,207
- We should just boil it.
- It's for soup?

178
00:08:32,208 --> 00:08:33,041
Oh, boiled chicken soup?

179
00:08:33,042 --> 00:08:34,576
And we have two blocks of tofu.

180
00:08:34,577 --> 00:08:36,477
To make the soup, we'd have to cook
it for a really long time.

181
00:08:36,478 --> 00:08:38,079
I think everyone's going
to get too hungry.

182
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,348
- I know.
- To make boiled chicken soup?

183
00:08:39,349 --> 00:08:40,215
Yes, to make the soup.

184
00:08:40,884 --> 00:08:43,385
- Then let's save this for tomorrow.
- Let's have it for breakfast.

185
00:08:43,386 --> 00:08:44,738
- Let's put it in the fridge.
- Let's do that.

186
00:08:46,989 --> 00:08:49,624
Should we have just ordered fried chicken?

187
00:08:49,625 --> 00:08:50,991
- It's too much trouble to cook now.
- I know.

188
00:08:50,992 --> 00:08:52,560
Then, how about this?

189
00:08:52,561 --> 00:08:55,764
Let's just order some fried chicken
and beer tonight.

190
00:08:55,765 --> 00:08:57,165
I think that's a good idea too.

191
00:08:58,835 --> 00:09:01,069
Six orders of fried chicken
and beer, please.

192
00:09:01,437 --> 00:09:02,270
Yes!

193
00:09:02,772 --> 00:09:04,272
Purchase complete.

194
00:09:04,273 --> 00:09:05,907
We're splurging today!

195
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:07,842
Now this is how you splurge!

196
00:09:08,110 --> 00:09:09,811
- I know.
- And we still have 10,000 dollars left!

197
00:09:09,812 --> 00:09:11,579
- Unbelievable!
- We won't have to shop for a while.

198
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,114
Until tomorrow or the day after.

199
00:09:13,115 --> 00:09:15,316
Yes, we have enough that we won't need
to buy groceries for another two days.

200
00:09:15,317 --> 00:09:17,052
I'll keep cooking
as long as I don't get eliminated.

201
00:09:19,055 --> 00:09:20,655
Just hang on three more days.

202
00:09:20,656 --> 00:09:22,591
We just need to keep our guard up
for three more days.

203
00:09:22,592 --> 00:09:24,026
Oh, we only have to hang on
for three more days?

204
00:09:24,027 --> 00:09:25,694
- Yes.
- There won't be any attacks on Day Six.

205
00:09:25,795 --> 00:09:28,597
Doesn't that mean
the attacks could start today?

206
00:09:29,031 --> 00:09:31,132
- I know.
- I think they will.

207
00:09:31,133 --> 00:09:33,735
Now that everyone has money,
they can actually afford to attack.

208
00:09:33,736 --> 00:09:36,404
I even thought about keeping watch
in front of that gate.

209
00:09:36,405 --> 00:09:37,805
Come to think of it, the spy needs...

210
00:09:37,806 --> 00:09:41,043
- money to launch an attack too,
- Of course.

211
00:09:41,044 --> 00:09:43,678
so they can't just make
endless random guesses.

212
00:09:43,679 --> 00:09:45,780
There's another way.

213
00:09:45,781 --> 00:09:47,115
The person who's being attacked...

214
00:09:47,483 --> 00:09:50,785
will be notified
on the screen in their room.

215
00:09:50,986 --> 00:09:52,420
Right. They'll find out.

216
00:09:52,421 --> 00:09:54,088
Then that person could tell us...

217
00:09:54,089 --> 00:09:56,024
before tomorrow morning.

218
00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:59,361
- Right?
- Right.

219
00:10:02,498 --> 00:10:04,098
What do you think is going
to happen from now on?

220
00:10:04,099 --> 00:10:06,668
I still have no idea what we'll be doing
on Days 7, 8, and 9.

221
00:10:06,669 --> 00:10:08,036
Will it turn into a team game?

222
00:10:08,371 --> 00:10:10,772
I actually think it'll become
an individual game.

223
00:10:10,773 --> 00:10:12,274
Eventually, it'll come down
to individuals.

224
00:10:12,275 --> 00:10:13,642
They'll probably make us take
each other's money.

225
00:10:13,643 --> 00:10:16,678
They'll give missions where we have
no choice but to steal each other's money.

226
00:10:16,679 --> 00:10:19,849
Maybe that's why they let
us earn so much today.

227
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:21,249
That's what I told the others earlier.

228
00:10:21,517 --> 00:10:23,451
That they'll hand out a lot
of money today.

229
00:10:23,986 --> 00:10:26,655
Because you need money to fight.

230
00:10:26,656 --> 00:10:28,022
How are you supposed to fight
if you're broke?

231
00:10:28,191 --> 00:10:29,491
You know what I was thinking?

232
00:10:29,926 --> 00:10:32,227
If none of us had money, we'd have
no choice but to get along well.

233
00:10:32,627 --> 00:10:34,195
We'd naturally stick together.

234
00:10:34,196 --> 00:10:35,029
You're right.

235
00:10:35,498 --> 00:10:37,032
People who have nothing
don't fight each other.

236
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:39,568
Right.
How could they?

237
00:10:39,569 --> 00:10:41,536
There's no real reason to fight.

238
00:10:41,537 --> 00:10:43,272
If there's nothing to take,
what are you fighting over?

239
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:45,207
Why do wars happen?

240
00:10:45,508 --> 00:10:47,142
People fight to take what others have.

241
00:10:47,143 --> 00:10:48,710
People with nothing
don't fight each other like that.

242
00:10:49,145 --> 00:10:50,612
Society itself is basically
one big battle anyway.

243
00:10:51,581 --> 00:10:53,949
Hey, Ben. Can I ask you something?

244
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:56,084
- Yes?
- Has she told you anything?

245
00:10:56,085 --> 00:10:59,420
I'm the one who brought up...

246
00:10:59,421 --> 00:11:02,323
the idea of the media being corrupt.

247
00:11:02,324 --> 00:11:04,158
- Yes.
- Yesterday.

248
00:11:04,159 --> 00:11:06,594
- A theory?
- I had a conspiracy in my mind.

249
00:11:06,996 --> 00:11:10,298
I sincerely asked her...

250
00:11:10,299 --> 00:11:12,867
what she had in mind.

251
00:11:12,868 --> 00:11:16,104
But I got a lot of answers
that didn't really mean anything.

252
00:11:18,374 --> 00:11:19,574
Oh, shit.

253
00:11:20,109 --> 00:11:22,911
It could be this.
The deal she made yesterday...

254
00:11:22,912 --> 00:11:25,914
I think she may have been given
some kind of offer, possibly,

255
00:11:25,915 --> 00:11:28,216
between the dark side and the light side.

256
00:11:28,217 --> 00:11:30,118
But she chose the dark side.

257
00:11:30,119 --> 00:11:32,787
And if she doesn't keep that deal, or...

258
00:11:33,155 --> 00:11:35,189
If for some reason,

259
00:11:35,190 --> 00:11:39,268
the spy fails
to do his or her mission today,

260
00:11:39,335 --> 00:11:41,796
then she could be cut off.

261
00:11:42,531 --> 00:11:44,199
That's one possibility.

262
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:47,835
From now on, maybe we should just leave
the Reporter out entirely.

263
00:11:47,836 --> 00:11:50,638
Even if she's not the spy, at this point,
she's practically a second spy.

264
00:11:50,639 --> 00:11:53,208
It feels like she's almost helping
the spy.

265
00:11:53,209 --> 00:11:56,511
That's true. It's just made everything
even more confusing.

266
00:12:11,961 --> 00:12:13,762
What is it?

267
00:12:14,196 --> 00:12:16,130
- Thanks, Nangja.
- Sure.

268
00:12:16,298 --> 00:12:19,200
- My goodness. Good work today.
- You too. You went through a lot.

269
00:12:19,201 --> 00:12:21,168
Maybe I should grab one too.

270
00:12:22,538 --> 00:12:23,505
Nangja.

271
00:12:24,173 --> 00:12:25,406
Are you sure you're okay?

272
00:12:25,407 --> 00:12:27,075
You look so stressed out.

273
00:12:27,276 --> 00:12:30,678
I had my own idea of how I wanted
to play the Reporter role,

274
00:12:31,515 --> 00:12:33,448
so I put an embargo in place.

275
00:12:33,449 --> 00:12:36,518
Once the embargo is lifted later,
I can talk about it.

276
00:12:36,519 --> 00:12:40,956
What I can tell you is...

277
00:12:40,957 --> 00:12:45,627
that a lot of people are starting to get
a little suspicious.

278
00:12:45,628 --> 00:12:48,296
- Of course. They have their reasons.
- So that's why...

279
00:12:48,564 --> 00:12:50,899
That's why I asked you this.

280
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:53,948
Whether they might let you ask
more questions...

281
00:12:53,972 --> 00:12:55,803
before the ideology check.

282
00:12:56,005 --> 00:12:58,773
And whether it's possible for you
to lift the embargo then.

283
00:13:00,042 --> 00:13:01,542
So right now, I think...

284
00:13:01,744 --> 00:13:03,545
I don't know.
Just for your best interest,

285
00:13:03,546 --> 00:13:08,283
just try opening up more to the people.

286
00:13:09,017 --> 00:13:11,386
I don't know.
I don't know what's going on, honestly.

287
00:13:11,387 --> 00:13:14,022
I just wanted to tell you
out of my concern for you.

288
00:13:14,023 --> 00:13:15,423
I totally understand
where you're coming from.

289
00:13:15,424 --> 00:13:17,358
I'd do the same
if I were in your position.

290
00:13:17,359 --> 00:13:18,559
Saying, "We chose you,"

291
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:21,062
"so take the questions we wrote down,"

292
00:13:21,063 --> 00:13:22,931
"ask them, and bring us the answers."

293
00:13:22,932 --> 00:13:26,333
- That's so...
- Nobody can force you to do that.

294
00:13:26,334 --> 00:13:28,169
That's basically socialism,
if you think about it.

295
00:13:28,170 --> 00:13:31,940
But if you think about the role
the media actually plays in society,

296
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:34,876
there's a reason for embargoing
information like this.

297
00:13:34,877 --> 00:13:35,743
Right.

298
00:13:36,245 --> 00:13:38,813
All the resentment and complaints being
directed at me...

299
00:13:38,814 --> 00:13:40,215
since yesterday come down to one thing.

300
00:13:40,216 --> 00:13:41,616
They chose me as the Reporter,

301
00:13:41,641 --> 00:13:45,043
but I'm not acting the way
they want me to. That's really all it is.

302
00:13:45,187 --> 00:13:46,988
But from what I understood yesterday,

303
00:13:46,989 --> 00:13:49,558
the Reporter isn't obligated to disclose
the information they have.

304
00:13:49,559 --> 00:13:50,826
They decide whether or not to reveal it.

305
00:13:51,093 --> 00:13:53,094
"The Reporter may choose..."

306
00:13:53,095 --> 00:13:56,264
"what information they verify
and whether or not to make it public."

307
00:13:56,265 --> 00:13:58,399
People usually choose the safest option.

308
00:13:58,767 --> 00:14:01,869
They treat each other politely
and avoid betraying one another.

309
00:14:02,338 --> 00:14:04,572
But the moment someone betrays them,

310
00:14:04,573 --> 00:14:05,774
that person becomes the enemy.

311
00:14:05,775 --> 00:14:07,642
From then on,
they start going after that person.

312
00:14:07,643 --> 00:14:10,612
And that's exactly
what they're showing us right now.

313
00:14:14,183 --> 00:14:16,584
I mean, Ae Shin...

314
00:14:17,119 --> 00:14:19,053
She keeps suggesting that we should put...

315
00:14:19,054 --> 00:14:20,555
forward two candidates from our side.

316
00:14:20,556 --> 00:14:24,926
Ted agrees, but he says he'll only commit
if the situation calls for it.

317
00:14:25,361 --> 00:14:26,962
I knew it wouldn't be easy.

318
00:14:27,630 --> 00:14:29,531
Anyway, that's what Ae Shin wants.

319
00:14:29,532 --> 00:14:31,266
She wants Superman
and me to become candidates...

320
00:14:31,267 --> 00:14:34,937
and completely shut out
the other side's candidate.

321
00:14:34,938 --> 00:14:37,205
But Superman says no.
He doesn't want to.

322
00:14:37,872 --> 00:14:39,140
What do you think then?

323
00:14:39,241 --> 00:14:41,209
Personally,
I don't think it's a bad option.

324
00:14:41,210 --> 00:14:42,610
But since Superman already said no,

325
00:14:42,611 --> 00:14:45,646
it feels awkward for me
to keep pushing the idea.

326
00:14:47,217 --> 00:14:50,151
I'm still not exactly sure
why he's against it.

327
00:14:50,785 --> 00:14:52,587
The other alliance is clearly trying...

328
00:14:52,588 --> 00:14:55,256
to push Nangja into a corner.

329
00:14:55,525 --> 00:14:57,124
Dark Knight keeps...

330
00:14:57,125 --> 00:14:59,360
talking about Nangja that way.

331
00:14:59,628 --> 00:15:01,896
Earlier, he said,
"She's crying like that..."

332
00:15:01,897 --> 00:15:03,164
"because she's the spy."

333
00:15:03,165 --> 00:15:06,100
But judging by Hippo's expression,
I actually don't think it's Hippo.

334
00:15:06,101 --> 00:15:07,768
Exactly. It could be Dark Knight.

335
00:15:07,769 --> 00:15:11,138
You can tell Hippo genuinely likes...

336
00:15:11,139 --> 00:15:12,807
- the ideas I'm putting forward.
- Don't trust her.

337
00:15:13,042 --> 00:15:13,842
I shouldn't?

338
00:15:13,843 --> 00:15:15,410
Maybe I've been too naive all my life.

339
00:15:15,411 --> 00:15:16,444
Don't trust anyone.

340
00:15:16,445 --> 00:15:17,912
I've never met people like this before.

341
00:15:17,913 --> 00:15:20,649
She runs a community herself too,

342
00:15:20,650 --> 00:15:22,083
so she knows
what it's like to manage a group.

343
00:15:22,084 --> 00:15:24,019
I've never lied like this before, so...

344
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:25,920
Me neither.

345
00:15:25,921 --> 00:15:27,288
(The four-person alliance loses
confidence.)

346
00:15:27,289 --> 00:15:28,123
It's so hard.

347
00:15:29,518 --> 00:15:32,160
The items you ordered have been delivered.

348
00:15:34,063 --> 00:15:35,997
- There are seasoned ones!
- Seasoned fried chicken!

349
00:15:35,998 --> 00:15:37,866
I need steamed rice, no matter what!

350
00:15:38,234 --> 00:15:39,301
Who wants rice?

351
00:15:40,202 --> 00:15:41,536
- It's insanely good.
- Is it good?

352
00:15:41,537 --> 00:15:42,503
It's so good!

353
00:15:42,805 --> 00:15:44,338
This is legit fried chicken.

354
00:15:47,176 --> 00:15:48,009
It's really good.

355
00:15:48,978 --> 00:15:51,680
This is seriously delicious.

356
00:15:53,582 --> 00:15:55,487
It feels like everyone's eating a lot
for the first time in a while.

357
00:15:55,512 --> 00:15:57,184
So everyone was holding back
because we were poor?

358
00:15:58,053 --> 00:15:58,986
Because we were nervous.

359
00:15:59,655 --> 00:16:00,955
I think that's part of the reason too.

360
00:16:01,924 --> 00:16:03,124
Come to think of it...

361
00:16:03,125 --> 00:16:04,626
Seeing that healthy balance makes
it taste even better.

362
00:16:04,627 --> 00:16:05,627
Right.

363
00:16:13,969 --> 00:16:15,437
What I can tell you is...

364
00:16:15,438 --> 00:16:20,040
that a lot of people are starting to get
a little suspicious.

365
00:16:20,041 --> 00:16:21,976
- Of course. They have their reasons.
- So that's why...

366
00:16:25,381 --> 00:16:27,649
Nangja isn't here.

367
00:16:28,017 --> 00:16:29,622
(Dinner with everyone except Nangja)

368
00:16:29,656 --> 00:16:31,086
Two, four, six...

369
00:16:31,453 --> 00:16:33,220
You need pickled radish with this.

370
00:16:33,221 --> 00:16:35,256
Does anyone know where Nangja is?

371
00:16:35,491 --> 00:16:36,958
- She is...
- Nangja?

372
00:16:36,959 --> 00:16:38,293
She's stepped out for a while.

373
00:16:38,794 --> 00:16:43,698
She said she'd tell us
what the embargo was about at dinner.

374
00:16:44,133 --> 00:16:46,634
- She'll tell us?
- Yes, at dinner.

375
00:16:46,869 --> 00:16:48,203
She'll tell us at dinner?

376
00:16:50,172 --> 00:16:51,839
Is this going to give me
indigestion again?

377
00:16:58,881 --> 00:17:00,682
Nangja, come and eat something.

378
00:17:01,517 --> 00:17:03,183
I came to get you in case you didn't
know the food was here.

379
00:17:03,184 --> 00:17:05,786
- I knew it was here.
- Nangja, come and eat!

380
00:17:06,789 --> 00:17:09,090
Should I have one more piece or not?
Maybe I should.

381
00:17:09,091 --> 00:17:11,593
- Eat while you can.
- Eat as much as you want.

382
00:17:11,594 --> 00:17:12,627
Enjoy it while you can.

383
00:17:12,628 --> 00:17:14,095
Since the embargo will be lifted tonight.

384
00:17:14,596 --> 00:17:16,096
Right. It'll be lifted.

385
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:20,100
If you're ready...

386
00:17:21,704 --> 00:17:23,938
- She'll talk about the embargo, right?
- Yes.

387
00:17:24,773 --> 00:17:27,174
Let's hear from the Reporter, everyone.

388
00:17:28,377 --> 00:17:30,812
I couldn't find it last night,
but I found it in my pocket.

389
00:17:30,813 --> 00:17:34,048
The questions you wanted me
to ask last night.

390
00:17:34,316 --> 00:17:36,918
The ones Hippo wrote down for me.

391
00:17:37,586 --> 00:17:39,654
The first question I asked yesterday was,

392
00:17:40,289 --> 00:17:41,689
"Who is the spy?"

393
00:17:41,690 --> 00:17:45,927
(Who is the spy?)

394
00:17:46,164 --> 00:17:48,373
That information cannot be disclosed.

395
00:17:48,398 --> 00:17:50,865
Of course, they said,
"We cannot answer that."

396
00:17:51,834 --> 00:17:55,803
The second question was, "Who did the spy
conduct an ideology check on?"

397
00:17:55,804 --> 00:18:00,475
Who did the spy conduct
an ideology check on today?

398
00:18:00,676 --> 00:18:02,844
That information cannot be disclosed.

399
00:18:02,845 --> 00:18:03,979
"That information cannot be disclosed."

400
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:06,414
Third, "What benefit
does the spy receive..."

401
00:18:06,415 --> 00:18:08,016
"if they survive until the end?"

402
00:18:08,041 --> 00:18:09,574
Whose code did the spy...

403
00:18:10,285 --> 00:18:13,120
(Nangja asked a different question
from the one she was given.)

404
00:18:13,231 --> 00:18:14,531
They said, "We cannot answer that."

405
00:18:14,556 --> 00:18:15,456
What?

406
00:18:16,959 --> 00:18:19,360
- They can't answer that question either?
- That doesn't sound fair!

407
00:18:20,262 --> 00:18:23,497
The resident whose code
the spy viewed today is...

408
00:18:24,566 --> 00:18:27,268
Nangja.

409
00:18:29,071 --> 00:18:31,773
So I said,
"Then I'll ask my fourth question."

410
00:18:34,576 --> 00:18:37,745
But after I passed on one, they told me
I could only ask up to three questions.

411
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:39,747
That's totally new information!

412
00:18:39,748 --> 00:18:40,648
Only three questions?

413
00:18:40,649 --> 00:18:41,816
They didn't tell us that.

414
00:18:42,251 --> 00:18:44,452
I didn't know that either.

415
00:18:44,453 --> 00:18:45,954
It was new information to me too.

416
00:18:47,289 --> 00:18:50,591
I made up the part
about not being allowed...

417
00:18:50,592 --> 00:18:52,360
to ask more than three questions.

418
00:18:52,361 --> 00:18:55,630
Because no matter how many more questions
they wanted me to ask,

419
00:18:55,631 --> 00:18:57,932
I needed some kind
of safeguard for myself.

420
00:18:58,133 --> 00:18:59,500
They had no way of knowing the truth.

421
00:19:00,436 --> 00:19:02,103
She said she didn't get any answers.

422
00:19:02,104 --> 00:19:03,838
But after putting an embargo on it,

423
00:19:03,839 --> 00:19:07,308
dragging things out,

424
00:19:07,309 --> 00:19:09,244
and acting all serious,

425
00:19:09,245 --> 00:19:10,945
she comes back
with something that ridiculous.

426
00:19:10,946 --> 00:19:13,581
I couldn't believe what I was hearing.

427
00:19:14,549 --> 00:19:16,651
I'm sure she's hiding something.

428
00:19:16,952 --> 00:19:21,289
We don't even know if she actually asked
those questions in the first place.

429
00:19:21,290 --> 00:19:22,623
It's really confusing.

430
00:19:23,659 --> 00:19:24,692
What I wanted to do was...

431
00:19:25,329 --> 00:19:27,295
investigative reporting, honestly.

432
00:19:27,930 --> 00:19:29,597
I wanted to dig deeper.

433
00:19:30,533 --> 00:19:33,067
Instead of just coming back...

434
00:19:33,068 --> 00:19:34,535
and telling everyone
exactly what I'd learned,

435
00:19:35,103 --> 00:19:37,304
I thought that if I acted
like I'd known something...

436
00:19:38,073 --> 00:19:39,306
(Pressuring the spy by acting)

437
00:19:39,307 --> 00:19:41,642
- That's the stance you were taking?
- You thought you'd get a reaction.

438
00:19:41,643 --> 00:19:42,843
I thought I'd get some kind of reaction.

439
00:19:43,779 --> 00:19:45,980
It was a gamble on my part.

440
00:19:45,981 --> 00:19:47,915
And in the process,

441
00:19:47,916 --> 00:19:49,217
I tried a lot of different things today.

442
00:19:49,718 --> 00:19:52,320
Is there anything you want to ask or say?

443
00:19:52,321 --> 00:19:54,655
If there's a spy among us,

444
00:19:54,656 --> 00:19:57,425
I think they'd understand
that I'm sending them a signal.

445
00:19:58,260 --> 00:19:59,727
I just want to make sure.

446
00:20:00,162 --> 00:20:01,996
Is that really worth betting on?

447
00:20:02,531 --> 00:20:06,401
(Nangja tested the people she suspected.)

448
00:20:06,402 --> 00:20:08,569
I wanted to drag this out
for as long as possible.

449
00:20:09,371 --> 00:20:11,405
I thought the longer I kept it going,

450
00:20:11,406 --> 00:20:12,774
the more pressure I could put on the spy.

451
00:20:12,775 --> 00:20:15,911
But so far, I still don't know. Not yet.

452
00:20:15,912 --> 00:20:18,512
So you've spent all this time on it,

453
00:20:18,513 --> 00:20:19,714
but you haven't gotten anything.

454
00:20:20,883 --> 00:20:21,783
That makes sense.

455
00:20:21,784 --> 00:20:23,484
I get what you mean.

456
00:20:24,019 --> 00:20:25,553
Okay, I get it.

457
00:20:25,955 --> 00:20:27,221
But was that all just an act last night?

458
00:20:28,524 --> 00:20:30,325
Has she always been such an amazing actor?

459
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:34,061
I can't trust it because I haven't
actually seen anything for myself.

460
00:20:34,062 --> 00:20:36,230
In here, you can't just trust
what people say.

461
00:20:36,231 --> 00:20:37,732
You have to trust their actions...

462
00:20:37,866 --> 00:20:39,867
and what they actually show you.

463
00:20:40,168 --> 00:20:44,471
Words alone don't mean anything
in this situation.

464
00:20:45,807 --> 00:20:47,374
Okay. I understand.

465
00:20:48,277 --> 00:20:49,644
I don't completely understand it,

466
00:20:50,145 --> 00:20:51,745
but I guess we have to trust her.

467
00:20:51,746 --> 00:20:53,147
What else can we do?
She's the person we chose.

468
00:20:53,148 --> 00:20:55,583
At least as long as she's serving
as the Reporter,

469
00:20:55,584 --> 00:20:56,917
I'm going to trust her.

470
00:20:57,686 --> 00:20:58,686
That sounds reasonable.

471
00:20:59,054 --> 00:21:01,756
I could understand a lot of the reasoning
behind her actions.

472
00:21:01,757 --> 00:21:02,890
I thought, "If I'd come up..."

473
00:21:02,891 --> 00:21:06,160
"with that strategy,
I probably would've tried it too."

474
00:21:06,161 --> 00:21:07,561
It could still be a lie,

475
00:21:07,562 --> 00:21:10,197
but I do think that kind
of strategy is possible.

476
00:21:15,237 --> 00:21:16,837
Today, the spy viewed...

477
00:21:17,272 --> 00:21:20,507
one resident's full code.

478
00:21:20,508 --> 00:21:21,809
(Spy code access)

479
00:21:23,645 --> 00:21:26,313
Please enjoy your meal.

480
00:21:26,915 --> 00:21:28,682
You want us to enjoy our meal
after telling us that?

481
00:21:28,984 --> 00:21:31,752
The spy viewed another code today, right?

482
00:21:31,987 --> 00:21:33,120
So they get to learn...

483
00:21:33,355 --> 00:21:35,589
- one person's code every day.
- They get one every day.

484
00:21:35,590 --> 00:21:37,325
But what we still don't know is...

485
00:21:37,326 --> 00:21:40,161
whether the spy gets to choose
whose code to view or whether it's random.

486
00:21:40,162 --> 00:21:41,395
- Right.
- We don't know that yet.

487
00:21:41,396 --> 00:21:43,397
Whether the code is randomly assigned
or the spy gets to choose.

488
00:21:43,398 --> 00:21:45,200
So there is some risk,

489
00:21:45,201 --> 00:21:46,901
but what do you think
about spending money...

490
00:21:46,902 --> 00:21:49,503
to try the same method...

491
00:21:49,504 --> 00:21:51,673
we used yesterday one more time?

492
00:21:51,674 --> 00:21:53,040
- I don't think it's a bad idea.
- Sounds good.

493
00:21:53,041 --> 00:21:55,576
If we do that,
it'll cost 4,000 dollars per person.

494
00:21:55,577 --> 00:21:57,678
We should discuss it first.

495
00:21:58,013 --> 00:21:59,480
Of course, I'm expecting...

496
00:21:59,481 --> 00:22:01,582
at least half the people here
to oppose it today.

497
00:22:01,583 --> 00:22:04,652
If we spend 4,000 dollars now,
there's an opportunity cost.

498
00:22:04,653 --> 00:22:08,923
If the income-earning activities stop
from tomorrow,

499
00:22:08,924 --> 00:22:11,125
some people could end up
seriously short on money.

500
00:22:11,593 --> 00:22:13,894
Before we discuss whether to spend it,

501
00:22:14,062 --> 00:22:16,296
since we have three chances
to ask questions anyway,

502
00:22:16,297 --> 00:22:18,298
why don't we come up
with three questions first?

503
00:22:18,567 --> 00:22:20,701
Then we can look at the answers
and decide what to do from there.

504
00:22:20,702 --> 00:22:21,736
Sounds great.

505
00:22:22,170 --> 00:22:26,874
How about asking which residents' codes...

506
00:22:26,875 --> 00:22:28,642
the spy has viewed so far?

507
00:22:28,643 --> 00:22:30,044
But we already asked that.

508
00:22:30,045 --> 00:22:31,611
No, not what code the spy viewed.

509
00:22:31,612 --> 00:22:32,446
Which residents.

510
00:22:32,447 --> 00:22:33,814
(Nangja already asked that yesterday.)

511
00:22:33,815 --> 00:22:34,715
Was it a different question?

512
00:22:36,618 --> 00:22:37,885
It's important to know...

513
00:22:38,387 --> 00:22:41,422
whether the spy gets to choose...

514
00:22:41,423 --> 00:22:42,924
whose code they view.

515
00:22:43,392 --> 00:22:44,692
Because we're all sitting here,

516
00:22:44,693 --> 00:22:46,361
the spy is hearing
this entire conversation too.

517
00:22:46,362 --> 00:22:48,529
That means they'll probably
want to eliminate...

518
00:22:48,530 --> 00:22:50,231
the people who are actively trying...

519
00:22:50,232 --> 00:22:51,666
to narrow down who the spy is.

520
00:22:51,667 --> 00:22:52,667
Right.

521
00:22:52,668 --> 00:22:55,002
We really need to know this for sure.

522
00:22:55,003 --> 00:22:56,704
If we know the spy can choose,

523
00:22:56,705 --> 00:22:59,207
then we can predict who they'll target
and protect that person.

524
00:22:59,208 --> 00:23:01,676
I think that's really important.

525
00:23:04,179 --> 00:23:04,945
Time's up.

526
00:23:07,382 --> 00:23:10,451
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

527
00:23:10,452 --> 00:23:12,620
(Anonymous Chat)

528
00:23:12,621 --> 00:23:15,489
The anonymous chat will begin shortly.

529
00:23:16,758 --> 00:23:18,125
For now, let's focus on the chat.

530
00:23:22,764 --> 00:23:24,198
There won't be much time after this.

531
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:28,202
Just keep thinking about it
while keeping an eye on the chat.

532
00:23:28,203 --> 00:23:29,504
I think it's really important.

533
00:23:29,705 --> 00:23:31,439
First, the Reporter question is important.

534
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:34,308
And then, whether there's any way
to block an attack tonight.

535
00:23:34,943 --> 00:23:36,644
If we can figure out those two things,
the rest is...

536
00:23:36,645 --> 00:23:38,078
just voting and stuff,
so we should be okay.

537
00:23:38,079 --> 00:23:40,915
(Reporter question,
How to block an attack)

538
00:23:43,085 --> 00:23:45,353
I'm not convinced.

539
00:23:46,088 --> 00:23:48,222
She just wanted to get rid
of the risk of suspicion.

540
00:23:48,457 --> 00:23:52,827
There's no way to validate her excuse.

541
00:23:52,828 --> 00:23:55,563
I'm pretty surprised
that nobody felt short-changed.

542
00:23:55,564 --> 00:23:57,064
Right.

543
00:23:57,065 --> 00:24:00,101
I'll be right back.
Let's talk more about this later.

544
00:24:00,102 --> 00:24:01,969
All right. Catch you later.

545
00:24:04,607 --> 00:24:06,807
Who's the spy?

546
00:24:09,444 --> 00:24:12,279
There's no way it's Nangja.

547
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:14,848
It was way too obvious
that she was lying earlier.

548
00:24:16,384 --> 00:24:18,085
Nangja and Michael.

549
00:24:19,821 --> 00:24:21,155
Michael.

550
00:24:24,126 --> 00:24:26,794
I need to get rid of him.

551
00:24:31,466 --> 00:24:33,133
Should I get him first, then Nangja?

552
00:24:36,039 --> 00:24:38,940
And I already promised earlier
that if Superman gets targeted,

553
00:24:38,941 --> 00:24:40,741
I'll give him my immunity.

554
00:24:41,409 --> 00:24:47,414
(She had promised to transfer her immunity
to Superman.)

555
00:24:47,650 --> 00:24:50,851
But if Superman becomes the leader,
he'll get immunity anyway, right?

556
00:24:51,705 --> 00:24:53,439
Would he get another one on top of that?

557
00:24:53,934 --> 00:24:57,536
- Because he's the leader.
- Then you could get one in return.

558
00:24:57,592 --> 00:24:59,626
I think you need to make sure.

559
00:24:59,627 --> 00:25:01,495
I think I'm being too lenient.

560
00:25:04,499 --> 00:25:05,933
I knew it.

561
00:25:07,536 --> 00:25:08,636
(Gray watches Go Ae Shin's reaction.)

562
00:25:08,637 --> 00:25:09,804
I'll just go get my shoes.

563
00:25:10,539 --> 00:25:12,340
The reason I told you
to keep an eye on Go Ae Shin is...

564
00:25:12,507 --> 00:25:15,509
because she seemed really discouraged
and overwhelmed.

565
00:25:15,510 --> 00:25:18,780
That whole immunity issue
probably hit her pretty hard.

566
00:25:18,781 --> 00:25:20,814
She's worried about getting pushed aside,

567
00:25:20,815 --> 00:25:23,951
so when she comes back later, make sure
she feels like she can really trust us.

568
00:25:25,453 --> 00:25:28,255
Superman isn't going to be easy.

569
00:25:29,224 --> 00:25:31,859
Go Ae Shin is anxious.

570
00:25:36,565 --> 00:25:39,367
If we succeed, we'll get all the money.

571
00:25:40,101 --> 00:25:42,135
Then someone might launch an attack now.

572
00:25:42,571 --> 00:25:43,938
Because we all have money now.

573
00:25:48,276 --> 00:25:50,177
Doesn't that mean
the attacks could start today?

574
00:25:50,178 --> 00:25:53,080
I even thought about keeping watch
in front of that gate.

575
00:25:57,319 --> 00:26:00,921
The person who's being attacked
will be notified in their room.

576
00:26:00,922 --> 00:26:02,523
Right. They'll find out.

577
00:26:02,524 --> 00:26:04,158
Then that person could tell us...

578
00:26:04,159 --> 00:26:06,027
before tomorrow morning.

579
00:26:13,935 --> 00:26:15,168
It's a cruel way to do it.

580
00:26:15,403 --> 00:26:16,670
Okay.

581
00:26:16,671 --> 00:26:19,439
I came up with a way to save all of us,
even though it's cruel.

582
00:26:19,741 --> 00:26:21,542
But it really is cruel.

583
00:26:23,345 --> 00:26:27,048
Registration for today's debate will
begin now.

584
00:26:28,183 --> 00:26:31,218
The debate topic is as follows.

585
00:26:32,454 --> 00:26:35,656
"Portrayals of Korean-Chinese people
as criminals..."

586
00:26:36,130 --> 00:26:37,858
"should be removed from mass media."

587
00:26:38,793 --> 00:26:41,461
Today will be the final anonymous chat.

588
00:26:41,997 --> 00:26:46,801
All residents must participate
in the chat twice.

589
00:26:46,802 --> 00:26:48,970
Registration is first come, first served.

590
00:26:48,971 --> 00:26:52,740
Residents who have not yet completed
their two required sessions...

591
00:26:52,741 --> 00:26:56,744
must participate
regardless of their personal stance,

592
00:26:56,745 --> 00:27:00,915
even if they fail to sign up
for the side they agree with.

593
00:27:01,983 --> 00:27:04,718
That's too extreme,
saying they should be removed entirely.

594
00:27:04,862 --> 00:27:08,932
I think mass media is a form
of individual creative expression,

595
00:27:08,957 --> 00:27:12,593
and it's not really something
you can regulate.

596
00:27:12,594 --> 00:27:15,696
(Against: a1, Genie)

597
00:27:16,531 --> 00:27:18,532
When it comes to mass media
and art in general,

598
00:27:18,936 --> 00:27:21,836
should there really be certain areas
that are off limits?

599
00:27:22,437 --> 00:27:23,404
I'm against it.

600
00:27:23,405 --> 00:27:25,005
(Against: DIAMOND11, Ted)

601
00:27:26,174 --> 00:27:29,143
I think restricting
those portrayals would...

602
00:27:29,344 --> 00:27:30,778
infringe on freedom of expression.

603
00:27:30,779 --> 00:27:32,913
(Against: coco000033, Sugar)

604
00:27:36,018 --> 00:27:38,819
It's a very complicated issue
that can't really be reduced...

605
00:27:38,820 --> 00:27:40,354
to simply agreeing or disagreeing.

606
00:27:40,489 --> 00:27:42,222
But the only spot left was
on the For side,

607
00:27:42,223 --> 00:27:44,191
so I signed up for that side.

608
00:27:44,192 --> 00:27:46,360
I decided I should just be honest
about what I really thought.

609
00:27:46,385 --> 00:27:48,853
(For: kababmania82, Hippo)

610
00:27:49,340 --> 00:27:52,742
Should I say, "It should disappear
to protect human rights?"

611
00:27:52,767 --> 00:27:54,368
But why should it disappear?

612
00:27:54,369 --> 00:27:58,172
I thought banning those portrayals
would restrict creative freedom.

613
00:27:58,173 --> 00:28:01,975
I had to take the opposing side
even though I didn't want to.

614
00:28:02,177 --> 00:28:04,945
I had no choice.
It was the last day, after all.

615
00:28:05,256 --> 00:28:07,457
I really don't agree, though.

616
00:28:07,482 --> 00:28:08,382
(For: DCINSIDE, Michael)

617
00:28:09,451 --> 00:28:11,285
Wasn't the Against side red before?

618
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:13,187
This is driving me crazy.

619
00:28:14,522 --> 00:28:16,590
(For: hikkan16, Gray)

620
00:28:16,591 --> 00:28:17,757
Get out of here!

621
00:28:20,095 --> 00:28:22,830
(Mass media portrayals of Korean-Chinese
as criminals should be eliminated.)

622
00:28:22,831 --> 00:28:25,266
(For: Michael, Hippo, Gray,
Against: Genie, Sugar, Ted)

623
00:28:25,267 --> 00:28:27,434
The chat will now begin.

624
00:28:27,435 --> 00:28:29,170
Please remove your blindfolds.

625
00:28:34,876 --> 00:28:36,910
(Mass media portrayals of Korean-Chinese
as criminals should be eliminated.)

626
00:28:36,911 --> 00:28:38,612
(For, Against)

627
00:28:38,613 --> 00:28:41,114
(For: Gray, Michael, Hippo)

628
00:28:41,115 --> 00:28:43,283
(Against: Sugar, Genie, Ted)

629
00:28:43,718 --> 00:28:46,820
(The debate begins.)

630
00:28:47,055 --> 00:28:49,223
(Mass media portrayals of Korean-Chinese
as criminals should be eliminated.)

631
00:28:49,224 --> 00:28:53,127
(Some of you may have had
no choice but to take a certain side,)

632
00:28:53,128 --> 00:28:56,530
(so let's just focus on making the debate
as rich and meaningful as possible.)

633
00:28:56,531 --> 00:28:57,832
(Okay.)

634
00:28:57,833 --> 00:28:59,299
(Great.)

635
00:28:59,934 --> 00:29:03,536
(I want to be careful in how I say this,)

636
00:29:03,537 --> 00:29:06,873
(but mass media is still a form of art,)

637
00:29:06,874 --> 00:29:08,975
(so I don't think we can go as far...)

638
00:29:08,976 --> 00:29:11,244
(as saying certain portrayals should
disappear entirely.)

639
00:29:11,245 --> 00:29:13,747
(I agree.)

640
00:29:14,516 --> 00:29:18,319
(Some works are based on real events too,)

641
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:20,888
(so saying those portrayals should be
completely removed feels too extreme.)

642
00:29:21,756 --> 00:29:24,124
(Then how do you feel about the way...)

643
00:29:24,125 --> 00:29:26,927
(Korean-Chinese people are
actually portrayed in the media?)

644
00:29:26,928 --> 00:29:31,231
(It does seem like they're cast
as criminals most of the time.)

645
00:29:31,232 --> 00:29:34,635
(Their portrayals tend to be
pretty similar across the board.)

646
00:29:34,636 --> 00:29:39,206
(That kind of harmful representation
reinforces stereotypes.)

647
00:29:39,841 --> 00:29:42,376
(What do you think
about the Korean-Chinese people...)

648
00:29:42,377 --> 00:29:45,813
(who are negatively affected
by those portrayals?)

649
00:29:45,814 --> 00:29:48,148
(I still don't think
we can go so far as...)

650
00:29:48,149 --> 00:29:50,651
(to say those portrayals
should disappear entirely.)

651
00:29:51,286 --> 00:29:54,488
(The media portrays
all kinds of criminals,)

652
00:29:54,489 --> 00:29:56,824
(not just Korean-Chinese people.)

653
00:29:56,825 --> 00:30:00,264
(There are male offenders,
female offenders,)

654
00:30:00,295 --> 00:30:03,197
(white offenders, Black offenders,
and many others.)

655
00:30:03,198 --> 00:30:06,000
(If Korean-Chinese people are
simply one group among them,)

656
00:30:06,001 --> 00:30:09,588
(then saying, "You can't portray
Korean-Chinese people as criminals,)

657
00:30:09,589 --> 00:30:12,644
(but portraying Korean offenders is
fine," would feel very hypocritical.)

658
00:30:12,974 --> 00:30:16,110
(They can be portrayed as criminals,)

659
00:30:16,111 --> 00:30:19,180
(but there's definitely an issue
when Korean Chinese characters...)

660
00:30:19,181 --> 00:30:23,751
(are consistently reduced
to the same cliché.)

661
00:30:24,452 --> 00:30:29,312
The thing is, movies and shows
feature all kinds of criminals,

662
00:30:29,336 --> 00:30:31,425
not just Korean Chinese characters.

663
00:30:31,893 --> 00:30:35,195
For instance,
it'd be an overreaction to say,

664
00:30:35,196 --> 00:30:37,764
"Male criminals shouldn't be shown..."

665
00:30:37,899 --> 00:30:41,168
"because that makes all men
look like criminals."

666
00:30:41,169 --> 00:30:44,071
By that logic,
if it's fine to portray criminals...

667
00:30:44,072 --> 00:30:45,773
from other ethnic backgrounds,

668
00:30:45,774 --> 00:30:49,777
but suddenly unacceptable
when they're Korean Chinese,

669
00:30:49,778 --> 00:30:52,512
isn't that a double standard?
It's a huge leap in logic.

670
00:30:53,014 --> 00:30:55,815
What really matters is
how they're written.

671
00:30:55,816 --> 00:30:58,051
Take a Korean Chinese criminal character.

672
00:30:58,052 --> 00:31:00,520
If they have zero depth or personality...

673
00:31:00,688 --> 00:31:03,390
beyond just being a criminal,

674
00:31:03,458 --> 00:31:07,995
you're simply boxing
Korean Chinese people into a stereotype.

675
00:31:07,996 --> 00:31:13,400
You're just reinforcing
existing prejudices.

676
00:31:13,401 --> 00:31:14,935
To me, that's just...

677
00:31:15,270 --> 00:31:16,537
lazy writing.

678
00:31:16,704 --> 00:31:19,574
Before even calling it unethical,
it's just poorly made.

679
00:31:21,209 --> 00:31:25,512
(I think this ties into how women
are portrayed too.)

680
00:31:25,513 --> 00:31:27,748
(Like the classic
"Madonna-whore dichotomy,")

681
00:31:27,749 --> 00:31:30,183
(where female characters
are boxed into rigid tropes.)

682
00:31:30,184 --> 00:31:35,422
(Are you really comparing
Korean Chinese people to women here?)

683
00:31:35,957 --> 00:31:39,159
(I'm not putting them
in the exact same category.)

684
00:31:39,160 --> 00:31:42,529
(I mean the underlying issue is similar:
assuming...)

685
00:31:42,530 --> 00:31:45,365
(you already understand someone
based on a label.)

686
00:31:45,366 --> 00:31:48,135
(It happens when you reduce
someone to a trope...)

687
00:31:48,136 --> 00:31:51,138
(and stop trying to understand
who they actually are.)

688
00:31:51,839 --> 00:31:52,872
(Well...)

689
00:31:52,873 --> 00:31:58,044
(If a film featured
an ultra-violent white psychopath,)

690
00:31:58,045 --> 00:32:02,315
(would we call that morally wrong
and try to censor it?)

691
00:32:02,316 --> 00:32:04,250
(I don't think psycho-thrillers...)

692
00:32:04,251 --> 00:32:08,655
(build their main themes
around a specific ethnic culture.)

693
00:32:08,656 --> 00:32:10,771
(I'm against exploiting cultural stigma
just to sell content abroad.)

694
00:32:10,796 --> 00:32:11,714
(Capitalizing on cultural stigma)

695
00:32:11,738 --> 00:32:13,927
(commercially exploiting the stigma
attached to a specific culture)

696
00:32:13,928 --> 00:32:16,529
(Don't you think that's even worse...)

697
00:32:16,530 --> 00:32:19,098
(when directed at a minority group?)

698
00:32:19,367 --> 00:32:22,269
(In films featuring
Korean Chinese criminals,)

699
00:32:22,270 --> 00:32:25,672
(isn't the genre usually
crime noir rather...)

700
00:32:25,673 --> 00:32:27,941
(than a story actually
exploring their culture?)

701
00:32:27,942 --> 00:32:31,678
(If the issue is portraying them
as a criminal gang...)

702
00:32:31,679 --> 00:32:34,748
(or caught up in organized crime,)

703
00:32:34,749 --> 00:32:37,951
(does showing a white drug cartel
also count...)

704
00:32:37,952 --> 00:32:40,787
(as an attack on white people
as an ethnic group?)

705
00:32:40,788 --> 00:32:42,022
Man... This is tough.

706
00:32:44,258 --> 00:32:47,693
(Write a post)

707
00:32:47,694 --> 00:32:51,598
My post on the feed was
about race-swapping characters.

708
00:32:51,766 --> 00:32:53,767
Personally, I'm against it.

709
00:32:53,935 --> 00:32:55,102
It feels way too performative.

710
00:32:55,103 --> 00:32:58,306
I get the intent
behind political correctness,

711
00:32:58,307 --> 00:33:01,141
but I really disagree
with the way it's often handled.

712
00:33:01,342 --> 00:33:04,644
If representation
and Black visibility truly matter,

713
00:33:04,645 --> 00:33:07,647
wouldn't it make more sense
to create original stories...

714
00:33:07,648 --> 00:33:08,848
with Black leads?

715
00:33:09,050 --> 00:33:12,619
Why take an established character,
swap their race,

716
00:33:12,620 --> 00:33:15,355
and shoehorn the original story...

717
00:33:15,380 --> 00:33:16,647
into a PC checklist?

718
00:33:17,159 --> 00:33:20,994
(Anonymous: A debate on PC culture
sounds way more interesting.)

719
00:33:20,995 --> 00:33:25,265
(What do you all think about casting
a Black actress in "The Little Mermaid"?)

720
00:33:25,266 --> 00:33:28,368
(Mass media portrayals
of Korean Chinese as criminals must end.)

721
00:33:28,369 --> 00:33:33,207
(The Black Little Mermaid debate
just came up on the feed.)

722
00:33:33,208 --> 00:33:35,676
(I didn't care about the actress's race,)

723
00:33:35,677 --> 00:33:40,723
(I was just disappointed it didn't capture
the magic of the original.)

724
00:33:40,748 --> 00:33:45,719
(In the grand scheme of things,
diversity is worth striving for.)

725
00:33:45,720 --> 00:33:52,392
(Maybe it's better to build fresh,
compelling stories around that goal,)

726
00:33:52,393 --> 00:33:56,396
(while respecting
the legacy of classic works.)

727
00:33:56,397 --> 00:34:00,300
(I actually loved the new Little Mermaid.)

728
00:34:00,649 --> 00:34:03,504
"How did they feel about it?"
Let me double-check my spelling.

729
00:34:04,038 --> 00:34:07,874
(How do you think
the Black community felt about it?)

730
00:34:07,875 --> 00:34:10,866
(I think the response was
overwhelmingly positive,)

731
00:34:10,890 --> 00:34:12,312
(especially from young Black girls.)

732
00:34:12,313 --> 00:34:14,014
(They finally got to see a princess
who looked like them.)

733
00:34:14,015 --> 00:34:17,385
(Every princess before this
was always white.)

734
00:34:17,386 --> 00:34:20,509
(The problem is when forced PC
ends up demonizing the majority,)

735
00:34:20,533 --> 00:34:22,088
(it stops feeling like progress...)

736
00:34:22,089 --> 00:34:25,859
(and starts feeling like petty payback.)

737
00:34:25,860 --> 00:34:29,729
(When I watched it...)

738
00:34:31,357 --> 00:34:34,125
(From what I saw in the film...)

739
00:34:34,802 --> 00:34:37,871
Everything I just typed was
a complete lie!

740
00:34:38,473 --> 00:34:40,641
What was there to like about that movie?

741
00:34:42,009 --> 00:34:45,879
I honestly couldn't stand
the new Little Mermaid.

742
00:34:45,880 --> 00:34:50,650
To me, it felt like it ruined
pure childhood nostalgia.

743
00:34:50,651 --> 00:34:51,785
That's why I hated it.

744
00:34:52,911 --> 00:34:55,680
Ten minutes left in the chat.

745
00:34:58,994 --> 00:35:01,195
Let's steer it back
to the Korean Chinese topic.

746
00:35:02,764 --> 00:35:05,032
(By that logic, Korean Chinese kids...)

747
00:35:05,033 --> 00:35:08,936
(have grown up seeing themselves
only as criminals on screen.)

748
00:35:08,937 --> 00:35:13,040
(Isn't it time to show them
in a positive light?)

749
00:35:13,041 --> 00:35:16,844
(We need stories...)

750
00:35:16,845 --> 00:35:21,281
(about immigrants
rising above hardship...)

751
00:35:21,282 --> 00:35:26,086
(and becoming heroes
or people to look up to.)

752
00:35:26,954 --> 00:35:29,823
(After the war,
ethnic Koreans in Japan...)

753
00:35:29,824 --> 00:35:32,325
(had so few options
that many turned to organized crime...)

754
00:35:32,326 --> 00:35:35,461
(or the pachinko business...)

755
00:35:35,462 --> 00:35:38,865
(because mainstream society
shut them out.)

756
00:35:38,866 --> 00:35:43,536
(The novel "Pachinko" completely
reframed that history.)

757
00:35:43,537 --> 00:35:49,108
(It secured massive funding
and became a global hit series.)

758
00:35:49,109 --> 00:35:52,745
(It broke out
in the American market first.)

759
00:35:52,746 --> 00:35:56,349
(It wasn't funded by Japanese studios
or tailored to their market.)

760
00:35:56,350 --> 00:36:01,788
(I'll definitely check it out.
Sounds incredible.)

761
00:36:03,558 --> 00:36:04,992
You know what's funny?

762
00:36:05,226 --> 00:36:09,329
Having to argue the counterpoint...

763
00:36:09,330 --> 00:36:11,665
actually started winning me over.

764
00:36:11,666 --> 00:36:15,235
It was humbling. It made me realize
how deep my own biases run.

765
00:36:15,437 --> 00:36:19,473
Being on this show forces you
to look in the mirror,

766
00:36:19,498 --> 00:36:20,765
and it's been eye-opening.

767
00:36:20,942 --> 00:36:24,311
White or Latino mobsters
get romanticized...

768
00:36:24,336 --> 00:36:27,104
and made to look like badass antiheroes
all the time.

769
00:36:27,315 --> 00:36:30,918
But Korean Chinese characters?

770
00:36:30,919 --> 00:36:34,154
They never get that kind of treatment.

771
00:36:34,288 --> 00:36:37,457
It really made me wonder...

772
00:36:37,458 --> 00:36:40,427
why we never get stories
where a Korean Chinese character...

773
00:36:40,428 --> 00:36:42,318
starts out dirty, but gets an arc,

774
00:36:42,342 --> 00:36:44,597
someone who grows or finds redemption.

775
00:36:46,133 --> 00:36:49,236
(Korea is already home
to so many immigrants today.)

776
00:36:49,237 --> 00:36:51,705
(Look back just a few decades,)

777
00:36:51,706 --> 00:36:57,367
(and Koreans were the ones navigating
harsh, isolated lives overseas.)

778
00:36:58,146 --> 00:37:00,013
(Whenever I think of immigrants today,)

779
00:37:00,014 --> 00:37:02,104
(I wonder if older Korean enclaves
like LA's Koreatown...)

780
00:37:02,128 --> 00:37:04,818
(went through the same growing pains.)

781
00:37:04,819 --> 00:37:09,122
(Immigration brings
so much vitality to a culture.)

782
00:37:09,123 --> 00:37:10,857
(Starting with the food!)

783
00:37:11,726 --> 00:37:12,626
(Amen to that!)

784
00:37:12,627 --> 00:37:14,628
(The food alone makes it worth it!)

785
00:37:18,308 --> 00:37:20,251
(Running out of time here.)

786
00:37:20,276 --> 00:37:22,170
(Hikkan, want to chime in?)

787
00:37:22,171 --> 00:37:24,971
Seriously.
Hikkan is acting like a total spy.

788
00:37:24,972 --> 00:37:27,040
He barely said a word last round.

789
00:37:27,508 --> 00:37:29,676
Staying completely silent this time too?

790
00:37:31,279 --> 00:37:33,780
(I didn't really have a fresh perspective
to add, so I just stayed quiet.)

791
00:37:33,781 --> 00:37:35,382
(My bad, guys.)

792
00:37:35,383 --> 00:37:38,050
(I had to show up today, so...)

793
00:37:38,051 --> 00:37:40,053
I clocked in, but mentally checked out.

794
00:37:40,054 --> 00:37:43,490
We were required to participate
in two of today's four debates,

795
00:37:43,491 --> 00:37:46,260
so even though I wasn't confident
on this topic,

796
00:37:46,261 --> 00:37:48,428
I had no choice.

797
00:37:48,429 --> 00:37:54,167
But jumping into a debate
when you don't know your stuff is suicide,

798
00:37:54,168 --> 00:37:55,469
so I made up a little excuse.

799
00:37:55,470 --> 00:37:57,204
I blamed it on system lag...

800
00:37:57,205 --> 00:38:00,641
and connection issues,
then just lurked the whole time.

801
00:38:05,079 --> 00:38:07,047
(Anyway, that wraps our final chat.
Great job, everyone)

802
00:38:07,048 --> 00:38:08,948
The chat has ended.

803
00:38:08,949 --> 00:38:11,418
(The chat has ended.)

804
00:38:11,586 --> 00:38:15,589
(Voting begins.)

805
00:38:16,357 --> 00:38:17,924
(Superman votes Against.)

806
00:38:17,925 --> 00:38:18,858
I voted against.

807
00:38:18,859 --> 00:38:20,992
Still, it definitely gave me
food for thought.

808
00:38:20,993 --> 00:38:22,395
The Black casting controversy was...

809
00:38:22,396 --> 00:38:24,964
such a massive culture-war issue
that I followed it closely,

810
00:38:24,965 --> 00:38:26,667
but the Korean Chinese debate
hits closer to home,

811
00:38:26,668 --> 00:38:29,502
and I realized
I'd been completely blind to it.

812
00:38:29,503 --> 00:38:31,705
It gave me genuine insight
into how other people see it.

813
00:38:32,506 --> 00:38:34,006
(Nangja votes For.)

814
00:38:34,007 --> 00:38:37,743
If the narrative calls for that archetype,
fine, include it.

815
00:38:37,744 --> 00:38:40,013
But we've seen that same tired trope
a thousand times.

816
00:38:40,014 --> 00:38:42,782
Instead of running it into the ground,

817
00:38:42,783 --> 00:38:46,219
shouldn't we balance it out
with positive depictions once in a while?

818
00:38:47,488 --> 00:38:49,122
(Benjamin votes Against.)

819
00:38:49,123 --> 00:38:52,125
Such an extreme stance is
impossible to get behind.

820
00:38:52,126 --> 00:38:54,761
That line of thinking
borders on unconstitutional.

821
00:38:54,762 --> 00:38:58,431
How can anyone justify outright banning...

822
00:38:59,076 --> 00:39:02,335
a form of artistic expression
purely based on content?

823
00:39:02,336 --> 00:39:03,871
Once you greenlight
that level of restriction,

824
00:39:04,338 --> 00:39:07,874
it opens the floodgates
to outright censorship.

825
00:39:07,875 --> 00:39:10,265
If any demographic can claim offense,
people will start demanding...

826
00:39:10,289 --> 00:39:12,645
every negative portrayal across media
be erased.

827
00:39:12,646 --> 00:39:14,180
That's a line I won't cross.

828
00:39:14,248 --> 00:39:16,916
(Voted Against: Ted, Sugar, Genie)

829
00:39:16,917 --> 00:39:18,985
(Benjamin, Dark Knight)

830
00:39:19,186 --> 00:39:21,687
(Voted For: Nangja,
Go Ae Shin, Polar Bear)

831
00:39:21,688 --> 00:39:23,956
(Hippo, Michael, Gray)

832
00:39:23,957 --> 00:39:25,691
Honestly, MVP belongs to me.

833
00:39:25,692 --> 00:39:26,993
The vote ended...

834
00:39:27,628 --> 00:39:29,696
in a 6-6 dead heat,

835
00:39:29,697 --> 00:39:34,233
meaning the "For" side takes the win
thanks to the MVP tiebreaker.

836
00:39:35,936 --> 00:39:38,037
The debate MVP is...

837
00:39:38,439 --> 00:39:41,975
kebabmania82.

838
00:39:44,289 --> 00:39:47,490
Everyone on the winning side
receives 1,000 dollars,

839
00:39:47,515 --> 00:39:50,216
(82,000, 64,000, 64,000 dollars)

840
00:39:50,318 --> 00:39:54,454
while 1,000 dollars will be deducted
from everyone on the losing side.

841
00:39:54,455 --> 00:39:57,190
(63,000, 49,000, 6,000 dollars)

842
00:39:58,459 --> 00:40:01,394
Today's debate was genuinely engaging.

843
00:40:01,395 --> 00:40:03,830
Everyone was basically
on the same page on the core issue,

844
00:40:03,831 --> 00:40:05,532
so I figured, "Why not build on that..."

845
00:40:05,533 --> 00:40:07,700
"and branch out
into bigger cultural topics?"

846
00:40:07,701 --> 00:40:11,971
It ended up feeling more
like a natural roundtable discussion.

847
00:40:11,972 --> 00:40:14,689
It had a completely different energy
from the earlier chats,

848
00:40:14,713 --> 00:40:15,676
which made it great.

849
00:40:16,510 --> 00:40:20,980
The MVP takes home
a bonus of 1,000 dollars.

850
00:40:20,981 --> 00:40:22,215
(9,200 dollars)

851
00:40:22,216 --> 00:40:24,851
To be frank, I cared way more
about snagging MVP...

852
00:40:24,852 --> 00:40:27,053
than whether our side actually
won the debate.

853
00:40:27,054 --> 00:40:28,354
The conversation just clicked.

854
00:40:34,562 --> 00:40:36,129
(Genie)

855
00:40:51,378 --> 00:40:53,512
(Superman)

856
00:40:54,181 --> 00:40:56,582
(Polar Bear)

857
00:40:57,017 --> 00:40:59,385
(Go Ae Shin)

858
00:40:59,620 --> 00:41:01,621
(Gray)

859
00:41:12,433 --> 00:41:15,235
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

860
00:41:17,171 --> 00:41:21,007
The Community Center will close
in one hour.

861
00:41:22,142 --> 00:41:25,111
Please submit
both your favorability votes...

862
00:41:25,613 --> 00:41:28,982
and your endorsement
for the lifetime leader candidate.

863
00:41:32,721 --> 00:41:36,156
Ideology Check can also be completed...

864
00:41:36,357 --> 00:41:38,792
until the Community Center closes.

865
00:41:43,230 --> 00:41:47,900
Furthermore, today's leader, Go Ae Shin,

866
00:41:49,503 --> 00:41:51,637
please report to the Community Center
immediately...

867
00:41:52,038 --> 00:41:54,707
to return the leader's box.

868
00:41:54,708 --> 00:41:56,120
(Return the leader's box
and put on your resident badge.)

869
00:41:56,145 --> 00:41:56,809
(Elimination immunity remains active.)

870
00:41:57,077 --> 00:41:59,678
(Go Ae Shin's term as leader
has concluded.)

871
00:42:03,517 --> 00:42:05,218
This is it, the final showdown.

872
00:42:05,753 --> 00:42:07,020
My head is spinning.

873
00:42:07,021 --> 00:42:09,189
How are we supposed
to figure this out in one hour?

874
00:42:09,190 --> 00:42:11,091
- An hour is nowhere near enough.
- Way too tight.

875
00:42:11,092 --> 00:42:12,292
Tell me about it.

876
00:42:12,527 --> 00:42:13,993
Absolutely brutal.

877
00:42:14,562 --> 00:42:17,731
I think I have a game plan.

878
00:42:17,732 --> 00:42:21,168
- Really?
- Though it's a bit...

879
00:42:21,769 --> 00:42:23,136
Kind of extreme...

880
00:42:24,305 --> 00:42:26,539
A little totalitarian, honestly.

881
00:42:26,540 --> 00:42:28,041
For real? What is it?

882
00:42:28,409 --> 00:42:29,309
Nicely done.

883
00:42:31,278 --> 00:42:33,512
Just one hour left on the clock.

884
00:42:33,513 --> 00:42:34,647
Why is everyone taking so long?

885
00:42:34,648 --> 00:42:37,083
We have to lock in our endorsements
for the lifetime leader.

886
00:42:37,084 --> 00:42:39,386
Endorsements, favorability votes,
Ideology Check.

887
00:42:39,387 --> 00:42:41,254
The favorability vote is
every person for themselves.

888
00:42:41,255 --> 00:42:43,522
The real issue right now
is the leadership.

889
00:42:43,523 --> 00:42:45,358
Are you throwing your hat in the ring,
or voting individually?

890
00:42:45,359 --> 00:42:46,225
We at least need to know who's running.

891
00:42:46,226 --> 00:42:47,793
Let's get on the same page first.

892
00:42:48,462 --> 00:42:52,365
Give us five minutes to hash this out.

893
00:42:53,282 --> 00:42:56,050
Since I've already been talking it up,

894
00:42:56,203 --> 00:42:59,305
I want to cast my endorsement
for Ted and Black Bear.

895
00:42:59,640 --> 00:43:01,008
- She's Polar Bear.
- Right, my bad.

896
00:43:04,578 --> 00:43:06,879
- Both of them?
- Keep your endorsement, thanks.

897
00:43:06,880 --> 00:43:08,614
Fine, I'll switch it.

898
00:43:08,615 --> 00:43:10,517
Well, you're definitely not the spy.

899
00:43:11,552 --> 00:43:13,687
Ted gets my sole endorsement then.

900
00:43:14,121 --> 00:43:16,823
I'm backing Ted and Benjamin.

901
00:43:17,758 --> 00:43:19,892
Shouldn't we start by finding out...

902
00:43:19,893 --> 00:43:21,428
- who actually wants the job?
- Exactly.

903
00:43:21,429 --> 00:43:24,897
- Oh, really?
- Someone who genuinely wants the job.

904
00:43:24,898 --> 00:43:27,300
I already talked
to a few of you about this.

905
00:43:28,469 --> 00:43:30,203
I told you I wanted to run.

906
00:43:30,204 --> 00:43:31,037
Okay.

907
00:43:31,372 --> 00:43:32,872
So you're running.

908
00:43:32,873 --> 00:43:34,508
- I'll write your name down.
- Sounds good.

909
00:43:36,143 --> 00:43:37,076
Superman.

910
00:43:37,311 --> 00:43:38,879
Anyone else want to step up
as a candidate?

911
00:43:38,880 --> 00:43:40,913
It's a lifetime post,
so it could get pretty interesting.

912
00:43:42,576 --> 00:43:45,019
Don't just sit there endorsing people.
Throw your own hat in the ring.

913
00:43:45,786 --> 00:43:47,087
You don't have to nominate someone else.

914
00:43:47,388 --> 00:43:48,822
You want us to run ourselves?

915
00:43:50,090 --> 00:43:53,092
I mean, don't be shy.
The floor is open to anyone.

916
00:43:53,794 --> 00:43:56,429
I'm definitely not interested.

917
00:43:57,097 --> 00:43:58,397
Come on. Who wants to run?

918
00:43:58,565 --> 00:44:00,799
- Not me.
- Count me out too.

919
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:02,369
Can I put someone else forward, though?

920
00:44:02,903 --> 00:44:04,704
You can nominate whoever you like.

921
00:44:04,705 --> 00:44:06,505
- Go for it.
- Do whatever you want.

922
00:44:06,674 --> 00:44:08,341
I nominate Go Ae Shin for leader.

923
00:44:10,177 --> 00:44:12,879
(She nominates Go Ae Shin,
a member of the four-person alliance.)

924
00:44:13,279 --> 00:44:15,782
I put Go Ae Shin forward...

925
00:44:16,016 --> 00:44:18,785
because her ideology score is four,

926
00:44:18,786 --> 00:44:20,052
and from living together,

927
00:44:20,053 --> 00:44:23,689
I've noticed she doesn't really hold
any rigid political biases.

928
00:44:23,690 --> 00:44:25,958
She isn't stubborn either.

929
00:44:25,959 --> 00:44:28,695
Superman, on the other hand,
is completely unyielding.

930
00:44:29,130 --> 00:44:32,098
I mean, sure, some people might
call that strong leadership.

931
00:44:32,466 --> 00:44:35,234
But if he and I ever clash,

932
00:44:35,235 --> 00:44:37,036
things could get really ugly for me.

933
00:44:37,438 --> 00:44:40,640
With someone like Go Ae Shin at the helm,

934
00:44:40,808 --> 00:44:43,243
I figured everyone would have
a much easier time getting along.

935
00:44:44,011 --> 00:44:45,111
Well...

936
00:44:45,379 --> 00:44:46,914
I'm not really looking to be
a lifetime leader either.

937
00:44:46,915 --> 00:44:48,681
I'm just not cut out
for that kind of role.

938
00:44:49,450 --> 00:44:50,417
Why not?

939
00:44:51,452 --> 00:44:52,419
Forget it.

940
00:44:53,120 --> 00:44:54,187
What about you, Ted?

941
00:44:54,826 --> 00:44:56,899
- You'd make a good leader.
- Why so cautious?

942
00:44:56,924 --> 00:45:00,960
I'm really not the type to step up
and take charge.

943
00:45:00,961 --> 00:45:03,296
Normally, I'll volunteer
for just about anything.

944
00:45:03,297 --> 00:45:04,932
This one actually scares me a bit, though.

945
00:45:05,265 --> 00:45:07,443
We have no idea
what responsibilities await the leader.

946
00:45:08,036 --> 00:45:09,602
At this rate, isn't Superman
running unopposed?

947
00:45:09,603 --> 00:45:10,636
A one-man race.

948
00:45:10,637 --> 00:45:13,439
We need two candidates, not one.

949
00:45:16,410 --> 00:45:17,544
Well, if that's how it is,

950
00:45:18,278 --> 00:45:19,378
I'll step up.

951
00:45:20,647 --> 00:45:22,715
(Lifetime leader candidates:
1. Superman, 2. Go Ae Shin)

952
00:45:22,716 --> 00:45:25,616
All right then.
We can vote for either of these two.

953
00:45:25,617 --> 00:45:28,887
The reason I turned Hippo down
initially was...

954
00:45:28,888 --> 00:45:32,424
that I was backing Superman anyway,

955
00:45:32,425 --> 00:45:35,160
so running against him felt contradictory.

956
00:45:35,161 --> 00:45:37,096
After thinking it through, though,
I realized I could run for now,

957
00:45:37,097 --> 00:45:40,466
and we could always rally
behind one candidate later on.

958
00:45:40,467 --> 00:45:43,269
That's why I agreed to put my name in.

959
00:45:43,270 --> 00:45:46,006
Time's running out.
An idea came to me while we were talking.

960
00:45:47,074 --> 00:45:49,409
Hear me out on this.

961
00:45:49,410 --> 00:45:50,843
It's basically
a deterrent against attacks,

962
00:45:50,844 --> 00:45:52,045
so I'll keep it short.

963
00:45:52,645 --> 00:45:54,280
It's pretty authoritarian,

964
00:45:54,281 --> 00:45:55,848
and personally, I hate this sort of thing,

965
00:45:55,849 --> 00:45:58,252
but if there's any shot at keeping
everyone alive, this might be it.

966
00:45:58,520 --> 00:45:59,574
If we open our bedroom blinds,

967
00:45:59,598 --> 00:46:01,588
we have a clear view
of the entire Community Center.

968
00:46:05,859 --> 00:46:07,693
You can see everyone
just by opening the blinds?

969
00:46:07,694 --> 00:46:10,362
From that angle,
you can spot anyone walking in.

970
00:46:10,964 --> 00:46:12,865
So here's the plan.
Everyone stays in their room...

971
00:46:12,866 --> 00:46:15,002
with the blinds wide open,
watching that entrance.

972
00:46:18,605 --> 00:46:20,039
Ted's a genius.

973
00:46:42,830 --> 00:46:44,798
You really can monitor the whole thing.

974
00:46:44,898 --> 00:46:46,934
You can watch every single person
head over to vote.

975
00:46:52,606 --> 00:46:53,739
Unbelievable.

976
00:46:54,108 --> 00:46:55,942
They really don't miss a trick, do they?

977
00:46:55,943 --> 00:46:57,811
Then, under full surveillance,

978
00:46:57,812 --> 00:47:00,681
everyone heads down one by one,
room by room, starting with Room 1.

979
00:47:00,682 --> 00:47:02,982
You cast your endorsement,
cast your favorability vote,

980
00:47:03,317 --> 00:47:04,919
and if you happen to be the spy
wanting to run an ideology check,

981
00:47:04,943 --> 00:47:05,852
you do it right then.

982
00:47:05,853 --> 00:47:06,853
(Superman)

983
00:47:08,522 --> 00:47:09,589
You've got this, Nangja.

984
00:47:09,590 --> 00:47:10,556
(Nangja)

985
00:47:10,557 --> 00:47:12,926
Once you wrap up your business,
you head back out and straight upstairs.

986
00:47:13,128 --> 00:47:15,028
The second everyone sees
that person return to their room,

987
00:47:15,053 --> 00:47:17,388
if no attack alert pops up
on our monitors,

988
00:47:21,735 --> 00:47:22,702
it proves that person isn't the spy.

989
00:47:22,703 --> 00:47:23,736
(Clearing of having used
an ideology check)

990
00:47:23,737 --> 00:47:25,405
If an alert does flash on the screen,

991
00:47:25,606 --> 00:47:26,739
we know instantly who the spy is.

992
00:47:26,740 --> 00:47:27,840
(The spy would be caught red-handed
if they attacked.)

993
00:47:27,841 --> 00:47:29,742
Because of the turn order!

994
00:47:29,743 --> 00:47:32,311
- Once they walk out...
- Exactly!

995
00:47:32,312 --> 00:47:34,223
and get back to their room,
the next person goes.

996
00:47:34,248 --> 00:47:35,991
This is starting to feel
like a surveillance state.

997
00:47:36,016 --> 00:47:37,083
Honestly, it's brilliant!

998
00:47:37,651 --> 00:47:39,318
- That's genius.
- How did you even come up with that?

999
00:47:39,319 --> 00:47:40,487
Never would've crossed my mind!

1000
00:47:40,488 --> 00:47:41,854
I think it's a solid plan.

1001
00:47:42,723 --> 00:47:44,957
In a way, we essentially built...

1002
00:47:44,958 --> 00:47:49,629
our own little panopticon
where everyone watches everyone else.

1003
00:47:50,065 --> 00:47:52,498
It might have...

1004
00:47:52,499 --> 00:47:54,500
looked a little twisted from the outside,

1005
00:47:54,501 --> 00:47:55,736
but I felt it was necessary.

1006
00:47:56,803 --> 00:47:58,271
You guys are really grasping at straws.

1007
00:47:59,139 --> 00:48:00,172
This changes nothing.

1008
00:48:01,608 --> 00:48:02,976
I have no intention of attacking anyone.

1009
00:48:04,912 --> 00:48:07,080
Even under all this pressure,
I'm not playing your game, you idiots.

1010
00:48:07,581 --> 00:48:09,015
This is full-on socialism.

1011
00:48:10,017 --> 00:48:11,485
Do they actually think this is right?

1012
00:48:12,553 --> 00:48:14,121
Is this how we should be doing things?

1013
00:48:15,622 --> 00:48:17,658
So we're all supposed to sit here...

1014
00:48:18,158 --> 00:48:19,860
and track who votes for whom?

1015
00:48:20,961 --> 00:48:24,096
I wouldn't go as far
as calling it socialism.

1016
00:48:24,097 --> 00:48:26,399
It's not like it's actually
hurting anyone.

1017
00:48:26,867 --> 00:48:29,069
We're just keeping an eye out
through the window, that's all.

1018
00:48:29,403 --> 00:48:33,006
Calling it socialism just for having
people look out the window...

1019
00:48:33,507 --> 00:48:35,208
It really wasn't that deep.

1020
00:48:36,143 --> 00:48:37,243
Everyone still alive?

1021
00:48:37,678 --> 00:48:38,478
Yeah.

1022
00:48:38,479 --> 00:48:40,380
If anyone got hit, speak up right now.

1023
00:48:40,381 --> 00:48:41,981
All clear here.

1024
00:48:41,982 --> 00:48:42,982
You good?

1025
00:48:44,451 --> 00:48:45,284
You too?

1026
00:48:46,186 --> 00:48:47,019
You're good.

1027
00:48:47,888 --> 00:48:48,621
You're good.

1028
00:48:52,893 --> 00:48:54,694
Reporter, you're up.

1029
00:48:54,695 --> 00:48:56,408
Polar Bear, you're right next door to me?

1030
00:48:56,432 --> 00:48:57,097
Yeah.

1031
00:48:57,682 --> 00:48:58,639
So we're neighbors.

1032
00:48:58,664 --> 00:49:00,199
- Hey there.
- Hey.

1033
00:49:00,801 --> 00:49:01,601
Right on.

1034
00:49:03,503 --> 00:49:04,870
I'll decide once I'm in the booth.

1035
00:49:04,871 --> 00:49:05,971
Sounds good.

1036
00:49:05,972 --> 00:49:07,940
Don't beat yourself up
if you don't get an answer.

1037
00:49:08,141 --> 00:49:09,842
Total police state.

1038
00:49:09,843 --> 00:49:11,210
You've got this.

1039
00:49:28,194 --> 00:49:30,664
I'm the reporter.
I'm here to submit an inquiry.

1040
00:49:31,064 --> 00:49:33,432
Please state your question.

1041
00:49:37,337 --> 00:49:45,945
(Does the spy get to pick
whose code they check?)

1042
00:49:47,681 --> 00:49:50,916
Knowing whether the spy
gets to handpick...

1043
00:49:50,917 --> 00:49:53,185
whose code they see is critical.

1044
00:49:53,186 --> 00:49:54,253
Because...

1045
00:49:54,254 --> 00:49:55,997
that means they'll likely
target the people...

1046
00:49:56,022 --> 00:49:57,757
leading the charge to weed them out.

1047
00:49:57,782 --> 00:49:58,815
Right. Exactly.

1048
00:49:59,126 --> 00:50:03,930
(The question the group wanted answered)

1049
00:50:09,636 --> 00:50:13,172
(Can the spy and the reporter
join forces and cooperate?)

1050
00:50:13,173 --> 00:50:15,809
If I were in the reporter's shoes,
I'd want two questions answered.

1051
00:50:16,043 --> 00:50:19,145
Survive together until the end
and split the prize pool?

1052
00:50:19,479 --> 00:50:20,947
Is that actually an option on the table?

1053
00:50:21,181 --> 00:50:23,649
Or second, if the spy gets eliminated,

1054
00:50:23,650 --> 00:50:25,251
does the role die with them,

1055
00:50:25,786 --> 00:50:28,521
or does someone else inherit it?

1056
00:50:28,622 --> 00:50:30,724
If we could get straight answers
to those two questions,

1057
00:50:30,857 --> 00:50:33,360
the reporter and the spy wouldn't have
any reason to target each other.

1058
00:50:33,894 --> 00:50:38,030
(The question Benjamin told her to ask)

1059
00:50:38,365 --> 00:50:41,601
(The reporter may submit
one question per day.)

1060
00:50:43,870 --> 00:50:47,343
(The group's question: Does the spy
get to pick whose code they check?)

1061
00:50:47,344 --> 00:50:50,876
(Benjamin's question:
Can the spy and the reporter join forces?)

1062
00:50:52,813 --> 00:50:54,690
What's running through my mind
as the reporter is...

1063
00:50:54,715 --> 00:50:57,302
this society,
this community that elected me,

1064
00:50:57,326 --> 00:50:58,418
feels deeply unsettling.

1065
00:50:58,685 --> 00:50:59,985
I'm not convinced this is right.

1066
00:50:59,986 --> 00:51:02,521
The whole setup is functioning
like a surveillance state.

1067
00:51:02,522 --> 00:51:05,357
Everyone is forced
to fall in line, take turns,

1068
00:51:05,358 --> 00:51:08,827
get checked off one by one,
and cast votes under open scrutiny.

1069
00:51:08,828 --> 00:51:11,096
It's designed so you can't even opt out.

1070
00:51:11,398 --> 00:51:14,233
If you refuse to take part,
it's basically shouting, "I'm suspicious,"

1071
00:51:14,234 --> 00:51:16,068
or even, "I'm the spy."

1072
00:51:16,069 --> 00:51:18,104
That's the kind of machine
they've built here.

1073
00:51:18,105 --> 00:51:21,374
It actually gives me chills.

1074
00:51:21,375 --> 00:51:24,910
This entire apparatus is set up
to keep the reporter on a leash.

1075
00:51:25,278 --> 00:51:27,313
If I decide this system is rotten,

1076
00:51:27,314 --> 00:51:29,257
that things aren't operating
the way they should,

1077
00:51:29,282 --> 00:51:31,516
I have the power to push back.

1078
00:51:31,785 --> 00:51:34,220
Right now, I'm genuinely torn
between which side to back.

1079
00:51:42,996 --> 00:51:44,930
I'm the reporter.
I'm here to submit an inquiry.

1080
00:51:45,699 --> 00:51:48,067
Please state your question.

1081
00:51:48,769 --> 00:51:51,139
"Can the spy and the reporter..."

1082
00:51:51,140 --> 00:51:54,006
"form an alliance and cooperate?"

1083
00:51:56,009 --> 00:52:00,112
(Nangja goes with Benjamin's question.)

1084
00:52:01,581 --> 00:52:03,817
That falls outside the scope
of information we can provide.

1085
00:52:07,621 --> 00:52:11,023
Since that wasn't answered,
let me ask something else.

1086
00:52:13,794 --> 00:52:15,428
"If the spy is eliminated,"

1087
00:52:15,662 --> 00:52:19,565
"is the role gone for good?
Or does a new spy get chosen?"

1088
00:52:24,171 --> 00:52:26,705
A new spy will be chosen.

1089
00:52:29,709 --> 00:52:32,577
"A new spy will be chosen."

1090
00:52:33,246 --> 00:52:35,747
Understood. Thank you. Goodbye.

1091
00:52:41,087 --> 00:52:44,456
I think there's a reason
this place was designed like this.

1092
00:52:44,791 --> 00:52:46,191
- Like a panopticon.
- So we can keep eyes on each other.

1093
00:52:46,192 --> 00:52:47,292
Exactly, a panopticon.

1094
00:52:48,295 --> 00:52:50,730
That's how we're able
to monitor everyone like this.

1095
00:52:50,997 --> 00:52:54,299
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

1096
00:52:56,537 --> 00:52:59,972
The Community Center is now closed.

1097
00:53:01,342 --> 00:53:03,675
Superman has...

1098
00:53:04,344 --> 00:53:07,113
officially qualified as a candidate
for lifetime leader,

1099
00:53:07,914 --> 00:53:10,950
however, no other candidate reached
the required four endorsements,

1100
00:53:11,285 --> 00:53:13,919
and their registration has failed.

1101
00:53:13,920 --> 00:53:16,355
(Three for Go Ae Shin)

1102
00:53:16,356 --> 00:53:19,958
(Seven for Superman)

1103
00:53:19,959 --> 00:53:21,593
I back Superman.

1104
00:53:22,596 --> 00:53:25,197
(Two residents abstained.)

1105
00:53:25,399 --> 00:53:26,232
Now what?

1106
00:53:26,768 --> 00:53:28,901
- We start over?
- Candidate registration...

1107
00:53:29,169 --> 00:53:31,270
will be extended until tomorrow morning.

1108
00:53:31,838 --> 00:53:33,639
- Okay.
- We will announce...

1109
00:53:33,874 --> 00:53:36,743
the updated registration schedule
tomorrow morning.

1110
00:53:37,578 --> 00:53:39,578
So we really have to have two candidates.

1111
00:53:39,579 --> 00:53:42,114
There must be a reason
they insist on having exactly two.

1112
00:53:42,115 --> 00:53:44,750
This concludes the Community Center
announcement.

1113
00:53:46,019 --> 00:53:48,287
There has to be a point to that rule.

1114
00:53:49,556 --> 00:53:51,857
They wouldn't mandate two candidates
for lifetime leader for no reason.

1115
00:53:52,192 --> 00:53:53,058
Right?

1116
00:53:59,666 --> 00:54:02,668
(All activities for Day 4 have concluded.)

1117
00:54:02,669 --> 00:54:05,304
Hey everyone, Nangja just got back
from submitting the question.

1118
00:54:05,939 --> 00:54:07,139
Did you get an answer today?

1119
00:54:07,373 --> 00:54:10,175
Out of the options you gave me,

1120
00:54:10,176 --> 00:54:14,514
I asked whether the spy gets to pick
whose code they look at...

1121
00:54:14,515 --> 00:54:19,051
or if the system assigns it at random.

1122
00:54:22,956 --> 00:54:25,991
They said, "The spy can choose
whichever code they want to see."

1123
00:54:26,860 --> 00:54:27,893
Got it.

1124
00:54:28,595 --> 00:54:30,129
What does that question even tell us?

1125
00:54:30,130 --> 00:54:32,965
What are we supposed to do with that?

1126
00:54:32,966 --> 00:54:36,402
All that does is make people spiral into,

1127
00:54:36,403 --> 00:54:38,737
"They checked the reporter's code,"
and jump to conclusions.

1128
00:54:38,738 --> 00:54:39,838
It only breeds more paranoia.

1129
00:54:39,839 --> 00:54:42,374
So I just told them that was the answer.

1130
00:54:44,177 --> 00:54:45,811
That's a huge piece of the puzzle.

1131
00:54:45,812 --> 00:54:49,848
The fact that the spy can
handpick their target...

1132
00:54:50,450 --> 00:54:52,651
changes everything.

1133
00:54:52,652 --> 00:54:57,256
For starters, it makes our defense
way stronger.

1134
00:54:57,257 --> 00:54:58,590
Now we can actually build a strategy.

1135
00:54:58,591 --> 00:55:02,194
We can ask ourselves,
"If I were the spy, who would I target?"

1136
00:55:02,362 --> 00:55:06,265
We can even put measures in place
to shield those exact people.

1137
00:55:06,266 --> 00:55:08,367
First time I've been able
to enjoy a beer in peace in forever.

1138
00:55:08,368 --> 00:55:11,203
- Now this is the life.
- Seriously, tell me about it.

1139
00:55:21,414 --> 00:55:24,316
Something doesn't add up right now.

1140
00:55:24,317 --> 00:55:25,927
It's bizarre that she couldn't
even pull four votes.

1141
00:55:25,952 --> 00:55:28,186
- Someone sabotaged it on purpose.
- Exactly.

1142
00:55:28,187 --> 00:55:30,890
Does that mean they already have
someone else in mind?

1143
00:55:30,891 --> 00:55:32,124
- You.
- Me?

1144
00:55:32,593 --> 00:55:33,893
That way, they drive a wedge between us.

1145
00:55:34,027 --> 00:55:36,129
For now, let's look for a backup
within our own camp.

1146
00:55:36,162 --> 00:55:37,462
Yeah. Makes sense.

1147
00:55:38,531 --> 00:55:40,932
That way, sticking to our promise...

1148
00:55:40,933 --> 00:55:42,000
(Superman is wary
of Polar Bear making a run.)

1149
00:55:42,502 --> 00:55:44,440
What do you think is in store
for tomorrow?

1150
00:55:44,464 --> 00:55:45,138
Tomorrow?

1151
00:55:48,241 --> 00:55:50,209
Go Ae Shin looks visibly upset.

1152
00:55:50,210 --> 00:55:52,311
Maybe she felt blindsided or hurt...

1153
00:55:52,312 --> 00:55:56,682
when we asked her
to hand over the immunity.

1154
00:55:57,150 --> 00:55:59,251
What worries me
more right now, though, is...

1155
00:55:59,519 --> 00:56:01,542
that we had another viable candidate,

1156
00:56:01,566 --> 00:56:03,288
and they still couldn't even
make the ballot.

1157
00:56:03,523 --> 00:56:05,125
I honestly have no clue what's going on.

1158
00:56:05,191 --> 00:56:07,259
What doesn't sit right with me is...

1159
00:56:07,260 --> 00:56:09,728
Go Ae Shin falling short of four votes.

1160
00:56:10,397 --> 00:56:13,866
That means people deliberately held back.

1161
00:56:13,867 --> 00:56:17,136
I can't tell if they blocked her
because they didn't want her running,

1162
00:56:17,871 --> 00:56:21,140
or if they already have
someone else lined up.

1163
00:56:21,141 --> 00:56:23,142
You can't obsess over every single person
with hurt feelings.

1164
00:56:23,143 --> 00:56:26,212
Except this place isn't normal.
There are only twelve of us.

1165
00:56:26,213 --> 00:56:27,580
The second someone feels burned,

1166
00:56:27,581 --> 00:56:30,216
it's easy for them to think, "Why not me?"

1167
00:56:30,217 --> 00:56:32,017
"Why am I the one
taking a hit for the team?"

1168
00:56:32,018 --> 00:56:33,886
Taking on the lifetime leadership...

1169
00:56:33,887 --> 00:56:37,524
comes with a massive risk
of corrupting whoever holds it,

1170
00:56:37,549 --> 00:56:41,260
no matter how decent they were
going into it.

1171
00:56:41,361 --> 00:56:46,732
If that level of pressure lands squarely
on the lifetime leader,

1172
00:56:46,966 --> 00:56:48,510
can Superman actually handle that?

1173
00:56:48,535 --> 00:56:50,369
We have to make a move here...

1174
00:56:50,570 --> 00:56:53,238
- If that's the play, let's do it.
- Let's do it.

1175
00:56:53,239 --> 00:56:54,873
(The four-person alliance fails to steer
the election as planned.)

1176
00:56:58,244 --> 00:57:00,745
(Morning of Day 5)

1177
00:57:00,980 --> 00:57:02,414
Morning.

1178
00:57:02,415 --> 00:57:04,316
(The early risers)

1179
00:57:04,317 --> 00:57:06,952
- Morning.
- Good morning.

1180
00:57:08,588 --> 00:57:10,889
Everyone will be up soon,

1181
00:57:11,284 --> 00:57:12,860
so I'd better start getting
breakfast together.

1182
00:57:12,892 --> 00:57:14,192
The chicken from last night...

1183
00:57:14,193 --> 00:57:15,927
- I'll let it simmer a bit longer.
- Right, chicken soup for breakfast.

1184
00:57:15,928 --> 00:57:17,195
Needs more time on the stove?

1185
00:57:17,196 --> 00:57:18,129
Chicken soup?

1186
00:57:18,599 --> 00:57:21,166
We picked up the ingredients yesterday.

1187
00:57:21,167 --> 00:57:22,334
- Really?
- Chicken soup!

1188
00:57:22,335 --> 00:57:24,536
It just would've taken way too long
to finish last night.

1189
00:57:24,537 --> 00:57:26,840
- We're taking it to another level.
- Tell me about it. It's awesome.

1190
00:57:38,752 --> 00:57:40,688
- Morning, everyone.
- Good morning.

1191
00:57:40,689 --> 00:57:41,821
Morning.

1192
00:57:46,493 --> 00:57:48,127
Need a hand with anything?

1193
00:57:49,562 --> 00:57:51,464
I was turning this over
in my head yesterday.

1194
00:57:51,965 --> 00:57:55,334
You know how we were talking
about creating an opposition leader?

1195
00:57:55,836 --> 00:57:59,170
Whoever takes the loss in the election...

1196
00:57:59,672 --> 00:58:02,342
will probably step into that role
naturally anyway,

1197
00:58:02,575 --> 00:58:05,278
even without designing
an entire system around it.

1198
00:58:05,945 --> 00:58:08,780
So I don't know if we really
need to go head-to-head...

1199
00:58:08,781 --> 00:58:10,350
and draw battle lines like that,

1200
00:58:10,517 --> 00:58:13,185
making it look like,
"I backed you before,"

1201
00:58:13,186 --> 00:58:14,653
"but now I'm pulling my support."

1202
00:58:14,654 --> 00:58:15,922
Here's the thing, though...

1203
00:58:16,156 --> 00:58:18,124
I don't necessarily think
going that route is a bad move.

1204
00:58:18,125 --> 00:58:19,725
My real concern is...

1205
00:58:19,726 --> 00:58:22,294
- whether you and I really need to...
- Stick our necks out for nothing?

1206
00:58:22,529 --> 00:58:23,830
Exactly. That's the catch.

1207
00:58:23,831 --> 00:58:25,331
Need any help over here?

1208
00:58:26,298 --> 00:58:28,567
I was looking for something to do myself.

1209
00:58:29,302 --> 00:58:31,303
That's actually what kept me up
this morning.

1210
00:58:31,304 --> 00:58:34,140
Whether I should genuinely
run for lifetime leader.

1211
00:58:34,349 --> 00:58:36,508
(Last night)

1212
00:58:36,509 --> 00:58:39,544
If we let this ride, we're stuck
in this awkward middle ground.

1213
00:58:39,545 --> 00:58:40,696
And if Superman takes power,

1214
00:58:40,720 --> 00:58:42,648
we're going to end up
in direct opposition.

1215
00:58:43,050 --> 00:58:44,216
- You mean fight him head-on?
- Yeah.

1216
00:58:44,217 --> 00:58:45,317
- Us?
- Exactly.

1217
00:58:45,318 --> 00:58:46,318
Right.

1218
00:58:46,319 --> 00:58:47,853
If we're not aligned with him,

1219
00:58:47,854 --> 00:58:49,855
we won't have any other choice.

1220
00:58:50,089 --> 00:58:51,858
We've already bumped heads
plenty of times.

1221
00:58:52,458 --> 00:58:54,859
Honestly, I was getting
pretty uncomfortable...

1222
00:58:54,860 --> 00:58:56,294
seeing these factions
crystallize so early.

1223
00:58:56,295 --> 00:58:58,229
It's about time someone broke that up.

1224
00:58:58,431 --> 00:59:01,433
Just shoot straight with me.
Don't worry about the fallout.

1225
00:59:01,935 --> 00:59:05,337
All I need to know is
if you actually want the seat.

1226
00:59:05,338 --> 00:59:07,573
I was worried I'd be at a disadvantage.

1227
00:59:07,674 --> 00:59:11,677
From where I'm sitting, though,
you've played a much stronger game.

1228
00:59:13,046 --> 00:59:14,246
Let me mull it over.

1229
00:59:14,614 --> 00:59:15,647
This is tough.

1230
00:59:15,949 --> 00:59:17,250
Give me your answer in the morning.

1231
00:59:21,688 --> 00:59:23,389
At the beginning,

1232
00:59:23,390 --> 00:59:27,860
running for lifetime leader
wasn't even on my radar.

1233
00:59:27,861 --> 00:59:31,063
I'd already thrown my support
behind Superman,

1234
00:59:31,331 --> 00:59:35,100
thinking maybe we could form
a strategic partnership.

1235
00:59:35,101 --> 00:59:38,304
At the same time, though,
we're built completely differently,

1236
00:59:38,305 --> 00:59:41,173
and I kept wondering if we'd just
end up butting heads at every turn.

1237
00:59:41,341 --> 00:59:45,044
Could we actually work
toward a shared vision?

1238
00:59:45,045 --> 00:59:46,478
Let's not let the conversation
go off the rails.

1239
00:59:46,479 --> 00:59:50,783
(The two have locked horns since Day 1.)

1240
00:59:51,493 --> 00:59:55,054
Then that leaves the moderator
with zero purpose.

1241
00:59:55,254 --> 00:59:56,722
Every time something comes up,

1242
00:59:56,723 --> 00:59:59,624
we almost never see eye to eye.

1243
01:00:00,126 --> 01:00:01,760
If there's already this much friction,

1244
01:00:01,761 --> 01:00:04,474
does it even make sense for me
to stay in his corner?

1245
01:00:04,474 --> 01:00:06,343
Could I even keep backing him like that?

1246
01:00:06,344 --> 01:00:09,411
If I'm going to push back eventually,
better to do it now.

1247
01:00:10,681 --> 01:00:13,783
I think I'll roll with your plan today.

1248
01:00:13,784 --> 01:00:15,318
- The one you told me yesterday.
- Oh, that one.

1249
01:00:15,319 --> 01:00:16,186
Did you talk to him?

1250
01:00:16,211 --> 01:00:17,411
- Yeah.
- Sounds good to me.

1251
01:00:18,755 --> 01:00:19,555
Ben.

1252
01:00:19,957 --> 01:00:22,158
We couldn't even get two candidates
registered last night, right?

1253
01:00:22,159 --> 01:00:24,494
So I told Polar Bear
to throw her hat in the ring.

1254
01:00:24,795 --> 01:00:26,829
It'll be Superman vs. Polar Bear.

1255
01:00:26,830 --> 01:00:29,465
Politically, Polar Bear and I are...

1256
01:00:29,766 --> 01:00:31,700
on completely different wavelengths
from Superman.

1257
01:00:32,569 --> 01:00:36,739
She's honestly much better leadership
material than he is.

1258
01:00:37,207 --> 01:00:40,009
Is she coming from a different angle
than Superman?

1259
01:00:40,010 --> 01:00:42,444
You've seen him.

1260
01:00:42,713 --> 01:00:44,247
Superman's a bit...

1261
01:00:44,248 --> 01:00:47,150
- pretty pushy, and...
- Total strongman vibe.

1262
01:00:47,151 --> 01:00:49,552
stubborn too.

1263
01:00:49,553 --> 01:00:52,455
If he takes charge,
we're bound to butt heads constantly.

1264
01:00:52,456 --> 01:00:54,157
It kept me up all night thinking about it.

1265
01:00:54,158 --> 01:00:57,193
Well, fine by me. No complaints here.

1266
01:00:57,194 --> 01:00:58,261
You on board with this?

1267
01:00:58,495 --> 01:00:59,962
- Sure.
- All right.

1268
01:01:00,697 --> 01:01:01,630
Good.

1269
01:01:01,631 --> 01:01:03,932
Originally, the three of us were a bloc.

1270
01:01:04,267 --> 01:01:06,369
But Superman and Polar Bear
don't see eye to eye at all.

1271
01:01:06,370 --> 01:01:09,605
So yesterday,
I went to work on Polar Bear.

1272
01:01:09,606 --> 01:01:13,809
I could tell she had genuine ambitions
to lead,

1273
01:01:13,810 --> 01:01:15,611
so I really leaned on her to run.

1274
01:01:15,879 --> 01:01:18,013
Ideologically and in the way
we see the game,

1275
01:01:18,014 --> 01:01:21,650
Polar Bear and I are practically
in lockstep.

1276
01:01:21,651 --> 01:01:24,920
Down the road, when people look back
at how this race was orchestrated,

1277
01:01:25,089 --> 01:01:26,922
one name is going to stand out: Gray.

1278
01:01:27,090 --> 01:01:29,258
The kingmaker in this election...

1279
01:01:29,259 --> 01:01:30,759
is me. Gray.

1280
01:01:33,463 --> 01:01:35,831
I'm nominating Polar Bear today.

1281
01:01:36,199 --> 01:01:37,199
You two go head-to-head.

1282
01:01:37,902 --> 01:01:40,169
She brought that up with me this morning.

1283
01:01:41,839 --> 01:01:44,440
It feels kind of awkward for me
to pull out now.

1284
01:01:44,441 --> 01:01:47,009
Just go for it.
Are you afraid you'll lose?

1285
01:01:47,444 --> 01:01:49,412
Bailing now looks bad anyway.
So what if you take an L?

1286
01:01:50,214 --> 01:01:51,680
Running still feels
like an enormous burden.

1287
01:01:51,816 --> 01:01:54,651
Unless the groundwork is already laid,

1288
01:01:54,652 --> 01:01:55,818
I don't think I should run.

1289
01:01:56,053 --> 01:01:57,954
How can you guarantee the groundwork?
You can't control how people feel.

1290
01:01:57,955 --> 01:01:58,788
I know.

1291
01:01:58,789 --> 01:02:00,990
I could see running...

1292
01:02:01,625 --> 01:02:03,859
if the alliance was locked in...

1293
01:02:03,860 --> 01:02:05,927
and you knew for sure
where a block of votes was landing...

1294
01:02:06,096 --> 01:02:08,531
What if we guarantee a 4-4 split? You in?

1295
01:02:08,899 --> 01:02:09,998
Four to four?

1296
01:02:10,967 --> 01:02:12,200
In endorsements.

1297
01:02:17,774 --> 01:02:18,874
Let me sleep on it.

1298
01:02:19,709 --> 01:02:20,642
Let me know.

1299
01:02:21,845 --> 01:02:24,513
Honestly, if there was a way out,
I wanted to take it.

1300
01:02:24,615 --> 01:02:27,116
I was worried about fracturing our group
into two opposing camps.

1301
01:02:27,117 --> 01:02:32,321
Plus, with Gray openly backing Polar Bear,

1302
01:02:32,322 --> 01:02:34,690
it hit me right then...

1303
01:02:34,691 --> 01:02:37,325
just how much personal trust
she had built up with everyone.

1304
01:02:37,794 --> 01:02:40,295
That made me realize
I might actually be on the back foot.

1305
01:02:48,138 --> 01:02:49,005
(The announcement begins.)

1306
01:02:49,006 --> 01:02:53,009
Candidate registration for Lifetime Leader
was not completed last night.

1307
01:02:53,643 --> 01:02:55,210
Residents,

1308
01:02:55,211 --> 01:02:59,448
please submit your endorsements
for two candidates once again.

1309
01:02:59,983 --> 01:03:01,183
We're wiping the slate clean?

1310
01:03:01,184 --> 01:03:03,319
You will now have 90 minutes...

1311
01:03:03,620 --> 01:03:06,789
to submit your endorsements
for Lifetime Leader.

1312
01:03:08,058 --> 01:03:10,660
(Lifetime Leader Endorsements)

1313
01:03:10,994 --> 01:03:12,995
Let's narrow down the field.

1314
01:03:12,996 --> 01:03:15,030
Did anyone sleep on it...

1315
01:03:15,031 --> 01:03:17,166
- and decide to run?
- Right.

1316
01:03:17,501 --> 01:03:18,968
Are we reopening nominations?

1317
01:03:19,370 --> 01:03:21,604
Anyone else stepping up this time?

1318
01:03:37,387 --> 01:03:39,888
Gray and I talked, and if you run,
I'm pulling out.

1319
01:03:40,190 --> 01:03:42,358
- Why?
- You and Gray...

1320
01:03:42,359 --> 01:03:44,026
already have a bloc formed,

1321
01:03:44,027 --> 01:03:46,762
and people like Sugar aren't
exactly fans of mine.

1322
01:03:47,230 --> 01:03:48,897
That side will never vote for me.

1323
01:03:49,200 --> 01:03:51,500
If you'd told me beforehand,
I wouldn't have thrown my name in.

1324
01:03:51,735 --> 01:03:52,968
If you'd just been upfront from the start.

1325
01:03:52,969 --> 01:03:55,470
Instead, the second the notice dropped,
you declared you were running...

1326
01:03:55,605 --> 01:03:57,639
Then you should've just said
you wanted it!

1327
01:03:57,640 --> 01:04:00,208
- But if we both run...
- The way this is set up,

1328
01:04:00,209 --> 01:04:03,211
you take the crown,
and I'm left looking like a fool.

1329
01:04:03,212 --> 01:04:04,146
I can't sign off on that.

1330
01:04:04,214 --> 01:04:05,181
Just stay in the race.

1331
01:04:05,182 --> 01:04:07,049
You already put your name forward.
Why drop out now?

1332
01:04:07,251 --> 01:04:08,750
Look, here's where I stand.

1333
01:04:09,186 --> 01:04:10,720
If you run...

1334
01:04:10,721 --> 01:04:14,023
and I'm essentially playing
second fiddle to you,

1335
01:04:14,024 --> 01:04:15,158
I'm a hundred percent down for that.

1336
01:04:15,159 --> 01:04:17,160
But if I fall in line now and end up
breaking ranks later,

1337
01:04:17,161 --> 01:04:18,894
I look completely absurd.

1338
01:04:18,895 --> 01:04:20,096
It'll look like I rode your coattails...

1339
01:04:20,097 --> 01:04:21,531
only to turn around
and play dirty politics.

1340
01:04:21,532 --> 01:04:23,499
- I'm fine with that.
- If that's how it plays out,

1341
01:04:23,693 --> 01:04:25,308
I'd rather just run against you.

1342
01:04:25,309 --> 01:04:26,802
That way, one of us takes the lead,

1343
01:04:26,803 --> 01:04:28,804
and the other serves as the opposition.

1344
01:04:32,542 --> 01:04:34,409
We're still stuck
on the registration stage.

1345
01:04:34,410 --> 01:04:36,545
- What about it?
- There's just...

1346
01:04:36,913 --> 01:04:39,848
too much friction between us to keep
pretending we're one unified bloc...

1347
01:04:39,849 --> 01:04:40,916
Right.

1348
01:04:41,585 --> 01:04:42,919
- We're too far apart.
- Moving forward?

1349
01:04:42,920 --> 01:04:44,253
Sure, we share the same high-level goal...

1350
01:04:44,254 --> 01:04:46,055
of keeping everyone safe.

1351
01:04:46,056 --> 01:04:47,390
But I don't think...

1352
01:04:47,391 --> 01:04:50,459
I can keep playing the loyal soldier
in Superman's corner.

1353
01:04:50,694 --> 01:04:52,595
We disagree on way too much.

1354
01:04:52,596 --> 01:04:53,696
Honestly, the two of us butt heads
more than anyone else here.

1355
01:04:54,297 --> 01:04:56,431
If I back his campaign now...

1356
01:04:56,432 --> 01:04:58,200
and later go,
"Actually, we're not aligned,"

1357
01:04:58,868 --> 01:05:00,403
"I'm out,"

1358
01:05:00,404 --> 01:05:03,271
I come off looking erratic.

1359
01:05:03,272 --> 01:05:04,673
Totally hypocritical.

1360
01:05:04,674 --> 01:05:06,842
And I wouldn't have any real
moral high ground to jump ship either.

1361
01:05:07,978 --> 01:05:08,844
This is brutal.

1362
01:05:09,155 --> 01:05:11,156
He already has organic support,

1363
01:05:11,181 --> 01:05:13,082
so if he crashes and burns in the vote,

1364
01:05:13,107 --> 01:05:15,308
- it blows back on him hard.
- Won't be a pretty look.

1365
01:05:15,986 --> 01:05:17,853
Look, Superman,
you already had solid backing yesterday.

1366
01:05:18,121 --> 01:05:19,221
Yeah, but...

1367
01:05:19,222 --> 01:05:21,290
almost all of it came
from inside our own circle.

1368
01:05:21,858 --> 01:05:23,392
If this goes to a general vote,

1369
01:05:23,393 --> 01:05:26,395
you keep your original base intact.

1370
01:05:26,396 --> 01:05:28,130
Just don't poach Gray from me.

1371
01:05:28,765 --> 01:05:30,933
I'm not asking you to split
your core supporters.

1372
01:05:32,035 --> 01:05:35,771
I'll go out and rustle up
my own votes elsewhere.

1373
01:05:37,173 --> 01:05:39,708
- Clock's ticking.
- Let's just lock you two in first.

1374
01:05:39,709 --> 01:05:41,276
- Anyway...
- Fill Benjamin in too.

1375
01:05:41,277 --> 01:05:43,378
I touched base with him earlier.

1376
01:05:46,082 --> 01:05:48,617
Here's the deal.

1377
01:05:48,885 --> 01:05:51,320
Superman wants it even more now,

1378
01:05:51,321 --> 01:05:53,021
which is fine by me.

1379
01:05:53,022 --> 01:05:55,691
The allies who backed Superman before...

1380
01:05:55,692 --> 01:05:58,327
can stick with him.

1381
01:05:58,828 --> 01:06:02,631
Wait, Gray told me to back Polar Bear.

1382
01:06:03,099 --> 01:06:04,066
Wait, really?

1383
01:06:04,367 --> 01:06:05,500
I said sure.

1384
01:06:06,770 --> 01:06:07,737
Hold on.

1385
01:06:08,805 --> 01:06:11,106
We never agreed to that.

1386
01:06:11,274 --> 01:06:14,076
The only reason I backed Superman
yesterday...

1387
01:06:14,444 --> 01:06:15,386
was because you said so.

1388
01:06:18,248 --> 01:06:19,915
As long as you've got my back...

1389
01:06:20,083 --> 01:06:21,819
Of course. Goes without saying.

1390
01:06:22,919 --> 01:06:26,355
In that case, I'm good.

1391
01:06:27,357 --> 01:06:28,825
Think of it this way.

1392
01:06:28,826 --> 01:06:30,092
If there are three candidates,

1393
01:06:30,426 --> 01:06:32,461
then we, wait. No. Four...

1394
01:06:32,896 --> 01:06:34,329
Instead of rushing to endorse,

1395
01:06:34,330 --> 01:06:36,465
why don't we just agree on two candidates
right here?

1396
01:06:36,466 --> 01:06:39,100
- Settle on two candidates first?
- Exactly. Lock in two names,

1397
01:06:39,101 --> 01:06:41,703
then split our numbers down the middle...

1398
01:06:41,704 --> 01:06:43,238
- so both can clear registration.
- Got it.

1399
01:06:43,406 --> 01:06:44,506
Something...

1400
01:06:45,108 --> 01:06:46,642
faintly crossed my mind yesterday.

1401
01:06:46,643 --> 01:06:49,363
I've been reading the room,

1402
01:06:49,364 --> 01:06:52,939
assuming everyone genuinely wants
a collective survival route.

1403
01:06:53,349 --> 01:06:56,918
Maybe that assumption was so automatic...

1404
01:06:57,063 --> 01:06:59,631
that we completely overlooked
the chance...

1405
01:06:59,656 --> 01:07:00,956
someone might be thinking,

1406
01:07:00,957 --> 01:07:02,892
"Honestly, I'd rather play my own game."

1407
01:07:02,893 --> 01:07:04,527
If that's the case,
maybe rather than hashing it out publicly,

1408
01:07:04,528 --> 01:07:06,262
even the preliminary votes should
be a secret ballot.

1409
01:07:07,230 --> 01:07:09,600
Well, that's exactly why registration
crashed and burned yesterday.

1410
01:07:09,601 --> 01:07:11,433
And I think that tells us something.

1411
01:07:11,434 --> 01:07:12,601
- You think so?
- Yeah.

1412
01:07:13,203 --> 01:07:15,570
Yesterday, with Superman
and Go Ae Shin on the ballot,

1413
01:07:15,571 --> 01:07:17,772
I figured both could easily scrape
together four endorsements.

1414
01:07:18,074 --> 01:07:22,177
Instead, one cleared it,
and the other fell short.

1415
01:07:22,545 --> 01:07:25,981
That made me realize our minds aren't
as aligned as we assumed.

1416
01:07:25,982 --> 01:07:28,284
- Fair point.
- Definitely feels that way.

1417
01:07:28,451 --> 01:07:31,053
I think Polar Bear and I...

1418
01:07:31,054 --> 01:07:34,556
can both pull four endorsements
without an issue.

1419
01:07:35,191 --> 01:07:37,025
Why not just leave it up...

1420
01:07:37,260 --> 01:07:38,360
to everyone's personal discretion?

1421
01:07:38,361 --> 01:07:39,394
Let's just do that then.

1422
01:07:40,497 --> 01:07:43,332
(Lifetime Leader Endorsements)

1423
01:07:44,200 --> 01:07:46,201
Hippo,
I thought you were throwing your hat in.

1424
01:07:46,202 --> 01:07:47,903
- Thought you were running.
- Who, me?

1425
01:07:48,538 --> 01:07:51,940
I'm definitely better off
in a support role.

1426
01:07:55,311 --> 01:07:57,112
You're still in the running,
right, Go Ae Shin?

1427
01:07:57,113 --> 01:07:58,346
No, I'm passing.

1428
01:07:58,347 --> 01:07:59,748
- You're dropping out?
- Yeah.

1429
01:08:00,450 --> 01:08:01,483
Just like that?

1430
01:08:01,951 --> 01:08:04,653
- Not interested anymore?
- Nope.

1431
01:08:04,654 --> 01:08:06,321
(Lifetime Leader Candidates:
Superman and Polar Bear)

1432
01:08:06,322 --> 01:08:10,125
At that point, there was no
real alternative candidate on the table.

1433
01:08:10,326 --> 01:08:11,628
From where I was standing,

1434
01:08:11,629 --> 01:08:13,928
when push comes to shove,

1435
01:08:13,929 --> 01:08:16,731
Superman will always be willing
to sacrifice someone.

1436
01:08:16,732 --> 01:08:21,436
And if he stays this fixated
on a witch hunt for the spy,

1437
01:08:21,437 --> 01:08:23,805
he's only going to drive...

1438
01:08:23,806 --> 01:08:25,807
a deeper wedge into the group.

1439
01:08:25,808 --> 01:08:28,444
So I decided that instead
of letting that happen,

1440
01:08:28,445 --> 01:08:31,813
I needed to step up
and let the community decide.

1441
01:08:32,348 --> 01:08:34,783
Can we get a quick pitch
from the candidates?

1442
01:08:34,784 --> 01:08:36,084
- Just a couple of words each.
- Shall we?

1443
01:08:36,085 --> 01:08:37,485
Whatever you guys want to do.

1444
01:08:39,322 --> 01:08:42,157
I've thought long and hard about this.

1445
01:08:42,526 --> 01:08:46,929
There may come a time when what's best
for me isn't best for everyone.

1446
01:08:47,130 --> 01:08:49,765
If that moment comes,
I want you to know...

1447
01:08:49,766 --> 01:08:52,735
that I won't hesitate for a second...

1448
01:08:52,736 --> 01:08:55,504
to put the group ahead of myself.

1449
01:08:55,505 --> 01:08:57,573
And even if I lose this election...

1450
01:08:57,574 --> 01:08:59,141
and someone else takes charge,

1451
01:08:59,142 --> 01:09:01,243
I'll still keep speaking up...

1452
01:09:01,244 --> 01:09:05,013
and doing my part to help build...

1453
01:09:05,014 --> 01:09:06,248
a better community.

1454
01:09:09,652 --> 01:09:11,620
Looks like the race is already underway.

1455
01:09:12,956 --> 01:09:14,256
Picking up from where we left
off yesterday...

1456
01:09:15,158 --> 01:09:17,192
I have a single focus.

1457
01:09:17,193 --> 01:09:21,730
I'll use every bit of power
and responsibility given to me...

1458
01:09:22,431 --> 01:09:24,633
to protect your lives and your assets.

1459
01:09:24,968 --> 01:09:27,469
That is my core promise. Period.

1460
01:09:29,572 --> 01:09:31,106
Can we ask questions?

1461
01:09:31,107 --> 01:09:33,041
Of course, go right ahead.

1462
01:09:33,042 --> 01:09:34,442
Plenty of time for Q&A. Let's do it.

1463
01:09:34,443 --> 01:09:35,677
A question for Superman.

1464
01:09:35,678 --> 01:09:39,681
What concrete steps will you actually take
to protect us?

1465
01:09:39,682 --> 01:09:42,217
We can't predict
how the rules will evolve from here.

1466
01:09:42,218 --> 01:09:45,921
We also don't know what missions
will be thrown at us,

1467
01:09:45,922 --> 01:09:47,422
but the leader is bound
to be given real authority.

1468
01:09:47,423 --> 01:09:49,259
If it's a lifetime post,
I doubt they'll just...

1469
01:09:49,260 --> 01:09:51,760
hand out a single immunity pass
and call it a day.

1470
01:09:51,995 --> 01:09:53,862
They'll keep putting the leader
in positions...

1471
01:09:53,863 --> 01:09:55,431
where they have to make the call...

1472
01:09:55,556 --> 01:09:57,899
between looking out for themselves
or protecting the group.

1473
01:09:57,900 --> 01:10:00,402
Especially with game theory hanging
over everything.

1474
01:10:00,737 --> 01:10:02,204
The ultimate question
in game theory is always...

1475
01:10:02,205 --> 01:10:04,473
who will strike first
and betray the group.

1476
01:10:04,474 --> 01:10:06,108
(Who will turn on the rest first?)

1477
01:10:06,109 --> 01:10:08,644
So the cornerstone of the system
I build will be ensuring...

1478
01:10:08,645 --> 01:10:10,913
that no one has an incentive
to turn on the rest of us.

1479
01:10:11,281 --> 01:10:12,815
And if anyone does betray us,

1480
01:10:12,816 --> 01:10:15,757
I'll hunt them down and make them pay,
whatever it takes.

1481
01:10:16,686 --> 01:10:17,953
Hell of an answer.

1482
01:10:17,978 --> 01:10:19,746
That definitely answers the question.

1483
01:10:20,924 --> 01:10:22,291
Polar Bear,

1484
01:10:22,559 --> 01:10:25,094
what's your vision
for the rest of our time together?

1485
01:10:25,095 --> 01:10:27,511
What kind of community are you
trying to build,

1486
01:10:27,512 --> 01:10:29,448
and how does that compare
to Superman's platform?

1487
01:10:30,600 --> 01:10:32,668
Naturally, we see eye to eye
on several fronts.

1488
01:10:33,002 --> 01:10:35,737
At the most fundamental level,
we both want...

1489
01:10:35,738 --> 01:10:37,672
to protect everyone's survival and assets.

1490
01:10:37,673 --> 01:10:39,441
That matters just as much to me.

1491
01:10:39,442 --> 01:10:40,942
Where we differ is the approach.

1492
01:10:40,943 --> 01:10:46,181
He emphasizes discipline, hunting down
the spy, and dishing out punishment.

1493
01:10:46,449 --> 01:10:48,717
Lately, though, I've started wondering.

1494
01:10:49,018 --> 01:10:52,887
Is the spy truly just biding their time
to blow things up,

1495
01:10:52,888 --> 01:10:55,890
or is their objective...

1496
01:10:56,359 --> 01:10:57,860
maybe not...

1497
01:10:57,861 --> 01:10:59,761
- entirely malicious after all?
- I agree.

1498
01:10:59,762 --> 01:11:00,896
So as leader,

1499
01:11:01,164 --> 01:11:02,897
I see my primary duty...

1500
01:11:03,333 --> 01:11:07,069
as making sure nobody feels abandoned
by leadership,

1501
01:11:08,171 --> 01:11:11,306
and ensuring that no one is left...

1502
01:11:11,307 --> 01:11:14,209
isolated or left behind.

1503
01:11:14,777 --> 01:11:15,744
Thank you.

1504
01:11:16,379 --> 01:11:17,980
Appreciate the answers from both of you.

1505
01:11:19,449 --> 01:11:20,649
Here we go, then.

1506
01:11:20,817 --> 01:11:22,651
You're always first up.

1507
01:11:22,652 --> 01:11:23,786
(Lifetime Leader Endorsements)

1508
01:11:23,787 --> 01:11:24,953
First in line, every single time.

1509
01:11:25,688 --> 01:11:27,989
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for the Lifetime Leader.

1510
01:11:28,725 --> 01:11:30,826
(Superman)

1511
01:11:30,827 --> 01:11:33,329
Superman reached out to me beforehand.

1512
01:11:33,730 --> 01:11:34,763
He told me what he aimed to do.

1513
01:11:34,764 --> 01:11:38,400
He said he knew there'd be a time
when I could really stand out.

1514
01:11:38,534 --> 01:11:42,136
Superman was the one who approached me,
not Polar Bear.

1515
01:11:42,137 --> 01:11:43,740
That effort stuck with me.

1516
01:11:43,741 --> 01:11:45,373
It proved how badly he really wanted this.

1517
01:11:46,142 --> 01:11:48,243
My support goes to Superman.

1518
01:11:48,244 --> 01:11:50,712
(Dark Knight endorses Superman)

1519
01:11:50,780 --> 01:11:53,014
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for Lifetime Leader.

1520
01:11:53,683 --> 01:11:56,585
(Gray endorses Polar Bear.)

1521
01:11:57,020 --> 01:11:58,821
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for Lifetime Leader.

1522
01:11:59,056 --> 01:12:01,890
(Go Ae Shin endorses Superman.)

1523
01:12:01,891 --> 01:12:03,225
Our time here is running short.

1524
01:12:03,893 --> 01:12:05,493
If this community were set
to last for months,

1525
01:12:05,494 --> 01:12:07,195
I might see things differently.

1526
01:12:07,220 --> 01:12:09,788
Given our tight timeline, though,
Superman felt like...

1527
01:12:09,966 --> 01:12:11,566
the type who can make tough calls
on the fly...

1528
01:12:11,567 --> 01:12:14,736
and speak hard truths
without mincing words.

1529
01:12:15,071 --> 01:12:19,441
(Superman: Dark Knight, Go Ae Shin,
Polar Bear: Gray)

1530
01:12:20,043 --> 01:12:22,577
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for Lifetime Leader.

1531
01:12:22,578 --> 01:12:25,714
Polar Bear is on the younger side...

1532
01:12:25,715 --> 01:12:28,917
and hasn't had as many shots at something
of this scale.

1533
01:12:28,918 --> 01:12:33,422
Stepping up to a real leadership role,
even in an environment like this,

1534
01:12:33,423 --> 01:12:36,192
could be a huge growth experience for her.

1535
01:12:36,193 --> 01:12:39,762
I'm backing Polar Bear.

1536
01:12:39,763 --> 01:12:40,930
(Hippo endorses Polar Bear.)

1537
01:12:40,931 --> 01:12:42,965
(Current Tally:
Superman 2 vs. Polar Bear 2)

1538
01:12:43,166 --> 01:12:45,467
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for Lifetime Leader.

1539
01:12:45,468 --> 01:12:46,368
(Nangja)

1540
01:12:46,369 --> 01:12:48,370
I'm backing Superman.

1541
01:12:48,371 --> 01:12:52,341
"I'll make sure everyone walks away
with the maximum possible payout."

1542
01:12:52,342 --> 01:12:54,109
"Good point."

1543
01:12:54,210 --> 01:12:56,311
"Play it that way,"

1544
01:12:56,312 --> 01:12:59,047
"and the pot for whoever makes it
to the end gets that much bigger, right?"

1545
01:12:59,215 --> 01:13:00,849
"Hard to argue with that."

1546
01:13:01,752 --> 01:13:03,518
If we were out in the real world,

1547
01:13:03,519 --> 01:13:06,555
I probably would've sided with Superman...

1548
01:13:06,556 --> 01:13:08,890
on most counts.

1549
01:13:10,059 --> 01:13:12,327
Funny thing is, though,

1550
01:13:12,328 --> 01:13:14,329
the reality in here is
an entirely different beast.

1551
01:13:14,330 --> 01:13:18,433
When facing things
that directly impact me,

1552
01:13:18,434 --> 01:13:21,436
like my prize pot, staying in the game,

1553
01:13:21,437 --> 01:13:24,474
or the very real risk of getting cut
and sent packing tomorrow,

1554
01:13:24,475 --> 01:13:26,708
I find myself leaning toward Polar Bear.

1555
01:13:27,986 --> 01:13:29,987
(Superman:
Dark Knight, Go Ae Shin, Nangja)

1556
01:13:30,012 --> 01:13:31,646
(Polar Bear: Gray, Hippo, Sugar)

1557
01:13:33,249 --> 01:13:34,950
(Polar Bear)

1558
01:13:36,753 --> 01:13:39,554
I'm endorsing Polar Bear for the ballot.

1559
01:13:40,557 --> 01:13:44,259
She's easily the most transparent person
here, and the most consistent.

1560
01:13:44,260 --> 01:13:46,795
Michael, if you ever face elimination,
my immunity is yours.

1561
01:13:47,730 --> 01:13:50,098
(Polar Bear pledged her immunity pass
to save Michael if he faces elimination.)

1562
01:13:50,099 --> 01:13:52,634
She has every quality
you'd want in a leader.

1563
01:13:53,269 --> 01:13:54,402
(Michael)

1564
01:13:54,403 --> 01:13:57,505
(Current Tally:
Superman 3 vs. Polar Bear 4)

1565
01:14:00,409 --> 01:14:03,211
Attention residents,
an announcement from the Community Center.

1566
01:14:04,013 --> 01:14:06,348
Polar Bear has officially registered...

1567
01:14:06,349 --> 01:14:09,217
as the first candidate
for Lifetime Leader.

1568
01:14:09,218 --> 01:14:10,218
Thank you.

1569
01:14:12,255 --> 01:14:17,226
(Polar Bear is the first to officially
qualify for the ballot.)

1570
01:14:18,194 --> 01:14:20,162
Broadcasting it over the speakers
so everyone hears.

1571
01:14:20,763 --> 01:14:21,763
Didn't take long.

1572
01:14:27,370 --> 01:14:29,538
I'm here to cast my endorsement
for Lifetime Leader.

1573
01:14:30,740 --> 01:14:31,573
(Polar Bear)

1574
01:14:31,574 --> 01:14:35,511
The type of community Polar Bear wants
to create...

1575
01:14:35,512 --> 01:14:39,014
lines up almost identically
with my own vision.

1576
01:14:39,715 --> 01:14:43,484
(Superman remains
one endorsement shy of qualifying.)

1577
01:14:43,719 --> 01:14:46,254
For the lifetime post, I want...

1578
01:14:46,455 --> 01:14:48,689
Superman on the ballot as the alternative.

1579
01:14:49,292 --> 01:14:50,425
Honestly, I don't care who takes it.

1580
01:14:50,726 --> 01:14:52,360
We need two candidates regardless,

1581
01:14:52,361 --> 01:14:53,895
so why drag our feet and burn time?

1582
01:14:53,896 --> 01:14:54,929
Polar Bear is already on the board, right?

1583
01:14:54,930 --> 01:14:57,365
Let's lock Superman in too, wrap this up,
and move along.

1584
01:14:57,366 --> 01:14:59,667
(Benjamin and Genie endorse Superman.)

1585
01:15:03,406 --> 01:15:06,741
I've been curious for a while...

1586
01:15:06,742 --> 01:15:10,012
about how Superman would actually
run things.

1587
01:15:10,013 --> 01:15:13,949
Since he's shown so much drive
to take the wheel,

1588
01:15:13,950 --> 01:15:16,051
I figured it was worth letting him
take his shot.

1589
01:15:19,689 --> 01:15:22,658
Attention residents,
an announcement from the Community Center.

1590
01:15:23,960 --> 01:15:26,194
Superman has officially registered...

1591
01:15:26,362 --> 01:15:30,598
as the second candidate
for Lifetime Leader.

1592
01:15:33,236 --> 01:15:37,372
Candidate registration
for Lifetime Leader is now closed.

1593
01:15:38,474 --> 01:15:41,743
Today is campaign day: both candidates...

1594
01:15:42,278 --> 01:15:45,747
will present their platforms ahead
of tomorrow's Lifetime Leader election.

1595
01:15:47,416 --> 01:15:51,219
The election itinerary will be shared
shortly.

1596
01:15:52,622 --> 01:15:55,791
(Endorsements conclude)

1597
01:16:02,598 --> 01:16:04,232
Go Ae Shin.

1598
01:16:05,868 --> 01:16:06,868
Well, it is what it is.

1599
01:16:07,604 --> 01:16:08,837
Chips fall where they may.

1600
01:16:10,072 --> 01:16:11,607
He seems pretty on edge about this...

1601
01:16:11,608 --> 01:16:12,607
Definitely on edge.

1602
01:16:12,608 --> 01:16:14,876
Getting a bit combative, honestly.

1603
01:16:16,178 --> 01:16:18,045
Man, this is getting complicated.

1604
01:16:18,046 --> 01:16:21,716
What had me sweating was the chance...

1605
01:16:22,018 --> 01:16:24,219
that the other camp might pull
a wild card out of nowhere.

1606
01:16:24,220 --> 01:16:25,053
Right on the mark.

1607
01:16:25,054 --> 01:16:26,288
Like, what if they ran Nangja?

1608
01:16:26,656 --> 01:16:28,190
How would we even counter that?

1609
01:16:28,191 --> 01:16:29,225
- She's...
- Same here.

1610
01:16:29,226 --> 01:16:31,026
Frankly, I was expecting Hippo to step up.

1611
01:16:31,027 --> 01:16:32,794
- Even if the plan was...
- But she opted out right away.

1612
01:16:32,819 --> 01:16:34,954
to rally behind a single consensus
candidate later...

1613
01:16:35,097 --> 01:16:37,532
Honestly, the only reason
I got the nod yesterday...

1614
01:16:37,533 --> 01:16:40,468
was because people wanted someone neutral
with zero baggage.

1615
01:16:40,836 --> 01:16:43,137
There was definitely a reason
Hippo backed me yesterday too.

1616
01:16:43,539 --> 01:16:45,006
- Exactly.
- Makes sense, right?

1617
01:16:45,007 --> 01:16:48,343
Hippo picked me precisely
because she saw me as a blank slate.

1618
01:16:48,711 --> 01:16:49,979
Whoever the spy turns out to be,

1619
01:16:49,980 --> 01:16:52,614
my bet is they're just waiting
for the right moment to strike.

1620
01:16:56,986 --> 01:16:58,620
Wait, look. It's pouring outside.

1621
01:16:58,654 --> 01:17:00,088
It's really coming down.

1622
01:17:00,113 --> 01:17:02,381
Nothing beats hot chicken soup
on a rainy day like this.

1623
01:17:03,292 --> 01:17:04,726
Did they make chicken soup?

1624
01:17:04,727 --> 01:17:05,627
- Awesome!
- Hot chicken soup?

1625
01:17:05,628 --> 01:17:07,362
They're whipping it up in the kitchen
right now.

1626
01:17:07,897 --> 01:17:09,397
That sounds incredible.

1627
01:17:10,567 --> 01:17:12,467
Ever since yesterday,

1628
01:17:12,468 --> 01:17:14,336
everyone's spirits seem so much higher.

1629
01:17:14,337 --> 01:17:15,804
Is this what money does to people?

1630
01:17:16,472 --> 01:17:19,274
- Financial peace of mind.
- Exactly.

1631
01:17:19,275 --> 01:17:21,744
- Probably plays a big part.
- Definitely helps.

1632
01:17:21,745 --> 01:17:23,311
A little breathing room in your pocket
changes everything.

1633
01:17:24,647 --> 01:17:25,981
If anyone's running low, just holler.

1634
01:17:26,182 --> 01:17:27,649
You can't transfer funds here.

1635
01:17:28,184 --> 01:17:29,284
No direct transfers.

1636
01:17:29,285 --> 01:17:31,253
Actually, you can.

1637
01:17:31,254 --> 01:17:33,155
Just tell me your code later.

1638
01:17:33,456 --> 01:17:34,823
I'll take the whole stash.

1639
01:17:35,424 --> 01:17:37,425
- You can pass yours along.
- She's right!

1640
01:17:37,426 --> 01:17:39,360
You hand over an immunity pass
and take their cash in return.

1641
01:17:39,495 --> 01:17:41,596
So you pass them immunity,

1642
01:17:41,597 --> 01:17:42,931
get their code,

1643
01:17:42,932 --> 01:17:45,100
run an ideology check to wipe them out,
and pocket the cash.

1644
01:17:45,101 --> 01:17:46,401
So, money transfers are possible or not?

1645
01:17:46,402 --> 01:17:50,205
Say I hold immunity.
I could hand it to Go Ae Shin,

1646
01:17:50,573 --> 01:17:52,674
then trigger an ideology check...

1647
01:17:52,675 --> 01:17:54,376
- pull her code, and eliminate her.
- Then he pockets my money.

1648
01:17:54,377 --> 01:17:56,278
I take her whole balance,
but she's still in the game.

1649
01:17:56,279 --> 01:17:58,914
- I survive with a flat zero.
- Even with zero balance, she stays in.

1650
01:17:58,915 --> 01:18:00,716
- And I get all her money.
- If that's the play,

1651
01:18:00,717 --> 01:18:02,284
doesn't that basically make you immortal?

1652
01:18:03,786 --> 01:18:04,753
What's your deal?

1653
01:18:04,754 --> 01:18:07,757
- Caught the joke.
- Pretty good one, right?

1654
01:18:07,758 --> 01:18:08,823
Bro.

1655
01:18:11,394 --> 01:18:12,761
Can we chat right here?

1656
01:18:12,762 --> 01:18:14,629
Better to talk out in the open
where everyone can see.

1657
01:18:14,630 --> 01:18:15,430
Fair enough.

1658
01:18:18,434 --> 01:18:20,035
Safer this way.

1659
01:18:21,103 --> 01:18:22,670
Got something for me?

1660
01:18:23,205 --> 01:18:25,907
Yesterday, I ran that question by them
as we planned...

1661
01:18:25,908 --> 01:18:28,709
and got an answer.

1662
01:18:29,812 --> 01:18:32,213
I asked if the two could coexist,

1663
01:18:32,581 --> 01:18:35,650
and they told me,

1664
01:18:36,219 --> 01:18:38,075
"That information is outside the scope
of what we can provide."

1665
01:18:39,288 --> 01:18:41,756
To me, that means whether the two team up,

1666
01:18:41,757 --> 01:18:43,992
coexist, or play nice is...

1667
01:18:43,993 --> 01:18:46,461
entirely on us to figure out.

1668
01:18:47,630 --> 01:18:52,367
Got it.
If anyone asks what this is about...

1669
01:18:53,569 --> 01:18:56,704
I'm just apologizing to you.
That's the story.

1670
01:18:56,705 --> 01:18:57,572
Works.

1671
01:18:57,573 --> 01:19:01,476
I brought up that wild theory earlier,

1672
01:19:01,811 --> 01:19:05,981
so I'm owning up and apologizing.

1673
01:19:05,982 --> 01:19:07,582
That's all this is.

1674
01:19:08,651 --> 01:19:12,287
Huddling up like this is super sketchy.

1675
01:19:12,822 --> 01:19:14,489
Not at all.

1676
01:19:14,623 --> 01:19:16,290
I'll clear the air in a bit.

1677
01:19:16,459 --> 01:19:19,094
I'm about to blow everyone's suspicions
wide open.

1678
01:19:19,363 --> 01:19:23,965
Michael is eyeing us hard right now.

1679
01:19:25,601 --> 01:19:26,534
If you...

1680
01:19:26,869 --> 01:19:29,471
go the distance,

1681
01:19:29,605 --> 01:19:30,805
the spy's going to have plenty
of cards to play.

1682
01:19:31,373 --> 01:19:32,773
Just stay alive.

1683
01:19:33,687 --> 01:19:36,077
The spy will hold up their end.

1684
01:19:38,047 --> 01:19:39,280
I'll make sure you take the win.

1685
01:19:40,916 --> 01:19:43,284
- Look, my bad. Really.
- Drop it.

1686
01:19:43,719 --> 01:19:45,853
- Just...
- Still on about that?

1687
01:19:46,755 --> 01:19:48,723
I already apologized once, but...

1688
01:19:49,458 --> 01:19:51,893
- Thanks for hearing me out.
- Seriously, my bad.

1689
01:19:51,894 --> 01:19:53,060
Don't sweat it.

1690
01:19:56,332 --> 01:19:57,766
Smells amazing!

1691
01:19:58,300 --> 01:19:59,433
Look at that broth!

1692
01:19:59,835 --> 01:20:01,669
It's cooked to perfection.

1693
01:20:04,240 --> 01:20:05,807
Anyone here actually like chicken necks?

1694
01:20:06,375 --> 01:20:07,508
You into chicken necks?

1695
01:20:09,411 --> 01:20:10,678
Some people swear by them.

1696
01:20:11,380 --> 01:20:13,948
Why do people eat the neck
when literally every other cut exists?

1697
01:20:14,984 --> 01:20:16,184
Do you eat them?

1698
01:20:16,886 --> 01:20:18,553
Only when there's nothing else left.

1699
01:20:18,554 --> 01:20:19,453
Got it.

1700
01:20:19,454 --> 01:20:20,622
You're disqualified.

1701
01:20:21,390 --> 01:20:23,958
Chicken neck isn't really
a love-it-or-hate-it thing.

1702
01:20:23,959 --> 01:20:25,793
It's more like a tiny cult following...

1703
01:20:25,794 --> 01:20:26,961
while the majority of people hate it.

1704
01:20:26,962 --> 01:20:28,229
Pretty much.

1705
01:20:28,230 --> 01:20:30,865
Though that cult following
never shuts up about it.

1706
01:20:31,233 --> 01:20:32,967
Very vocal minority.

1707
01:20:34,336 --> 01:20:36,203
Looks ready to me.

1708
01:20:37,573 --> 01:20:38,773
Thanks for the meal!

1709
01:20:38,908 --> 01:20:40,642
- Dig in.
- Enjoy, everyone.

1710
01:20:42,178 --> 01:20:43,979
- Kimchi is here.
- Nice and clean.

1711
01:20:46,215 --> 01:20:48,349
- This is fantastic.
- You like it?

1712
01:20:48,350 --> 01:20:51,653
Big round of applause for Superman
for deboning the whole thing for us.

1713
01:20:51,654 --> 01:20:52,820
He really is...

1714
01:20:54,323 --> 01:20:55,523
- Incredible.
- Thank you.

1715
01:20:55,558 --> 01:20:56,591
Total dad energy.

1716
01:20:57,459 --> 01:20:58,359
Seriously.

1717
01:20:58,427 --> 01:21:02,130
Something struck me earlier.

1718
01:21:02,131 --> 01:21:06,300
If we want to make real progress here,

1719
01:21:06,602 --> 01:21:10,605
we have to figure out why we keep
circling back to square one.

1720
01:21:10,606 --> 01:21:13,942
To catch the spy, the very first step...

1721
01:21:14,410 --> 01:21:16,444
is laying everything out on the table...

1722
01:21:16,445 --> 01:21:18,179
and working through our theories together,

1723
01:21:18,180 --> 01:21:22,117
as an entire room.

1724
01:21:25,254 --> 01:21:27,922
The catch is,
pointing fingers publicly is awkward.

1725
01:21:28,057 --> 01:21:30,759
People get defensive
when they're singled out,

1726
01:21:31,060 --> 01:21:34,029
and the person calling them out
takes all the heat.

1727
01:21:34,030 --> 01:21:38,266
At first,
I figured anonymous posting was the fix.

1728
01:21:38,267 --> 01:21:39,701
Except anonymity opens
a whole other can of worms.

1729
01:21:40,269 --> 01:21:41,970
When no one has to sign their name,

1730
01:21:41,971 --> 01:21:44,672
people can spin narratives and steer
the room with zero accountability.

1731
01:21:46,201 --> 01:21:50,364
(Am I the only one who thinks the Reporter
might not be telling the truth?)

1732
01:21:50,365 --> 01:21:51,299
(Post anonymously)

1733
01:21:52,948 --> 01:21:55,082
(Same here.)

1734
01:21:55,083 --> 01:21:56,584
(Post anonymously)

1735
01:22:01,657 --> 01:22:03,308
(Did they say
if the Reporter is eliminated,)

1736
01:22:03,309 --> 01:22:04,621
(there won't be another Reporter?)

1737
01:22:04,622 --> 01:22:05,361
(Post anonymously)

1738
01:22:09,114 --> 01:22:11,910
(There won't be another Reporter
if they are eliminated.)

1739
01:22:11,911 --> 01:22:16,510
(Then is it also true that if the spy is
eliminated, there won't be another spy?)

1740
01:22:16,511 --> 01:22:17,538
(Post anonymously)

1741
01:22:23,813 --> 01:22:26,941
(I support the Reporter's convictions.)

1742
01:22:26,942 --> 01:22:28,232
(Post anonymously)

1743
01:22:29,858 --> 01:22:33,356
(Sincerely, The Spy)

1744
01:22:33,357 --> 01:22:34,263
(Post anonymously)

1745
01:22:41,330 --> 01:22:44,502
(Hang in there, Reporter.
Don't put too much pressure on yourself.)

1746
01:22:44,503 --> 01:22:45,700
(Post anonymously)

1747
01:22:48,890 --> 01:22:51,641
(Sincerely, The Spy)

1748
01:22:51,642 --> 01:22:52,475
(Post anonymously)

1749
01:22:54,103 --> 01:22:56,544
To the Reporter, "Sincerely, The Spy."

1750
01:22:56,879 --> 01:22:58,146
That's comedy gold.

1751
01:22:59,548 --> 01:23:02,283
Actually sparked an idea.

1752
01:23:02,284 --> 01:23:04,852
Now that the debate's wrapped anyway,

1753
01:23:04,853 --> 01:23:07,655
we should just...

1754
01:23:07,756 --> 01:23:10,825
unmask the usernames.
That's my vote, at least.

1755
01:23:13,429 --> 01:23:15,530
(After the first chat)

1756
01:23:15,531 --> 01:23:17,132
Karen3000 was being a total Karen.

1757
01:23:18,967 --> 01:23:20,935
Could definitely be the spy.

1758
01:23:20,936 --> 01:23:22,670
High chance.

1759
01:23:23,205 --> 01:23:24,338
So who's Karen?

1760
01:23:29,445 --> 01:23:32,380
I'm not totally sold
on unmasking everyone.

1761
01:23:32,381 --> 01:23:34,149
- Right.
- Honestly,

1762
01:23:34,416 --> 01:23:36,751
the anonymous posts are half the fun.

1763
01:23:36,752 --> 01:23:38,719
I kind of love
this weird online dynamic...

1764
01:23:38,720 --> 01:23:41,022
we've got going on.

1765
01:23:45,060 --> 01:23:48,062
Fair point.
That actually sharpens my thinking.

1766
01:23:48,332 --> 01:23:50,298
Rather than unmasking everyone
across the board,

1767
01:23:50,299 --> 01:23:51,866
anyone running an actual investigation...

1768
01:23:52,034 --> 01:23:54,302
shouldn't hide behind an alt.

1769
01:23:54,303 --> 01:23:55,970
Post it under your real ID.

1770
01:23:55,971 --> 01:23:57,739
Because a single person can astroturf...

1771
01:23:57,740 --> 01:23:59,641
and make it look
like a whole crowd agrees, right?

1772
01:23:59,642 --> 01:24:02,710
So you mean post on the feed
under our own usernames?

1773
01:24:02,711 --> 01:24:04,278
- Exactly, on the feed.
- No anonymous tag?

1774
01:24:04,279 --> 01:24:05,647
Yeah, just own your words.

1775
01:24:05,648 --> 01:24:08,149
I thought you meant stripping
our anonymity here.

1776
01:24:08,383 --> 01:24:10,884
Feels like we're slowly sliding...

1777
01:24:10,885 --> 01:24:12,086
into big-government territory.

1778
01:24:13,156 --> 01:24:16,491
- Totally.
- Rules on top of regulations...

1779
01:24:17,092 --> 01:24:18,793
Sharp observation. Agreed.

1780
01:24:19,294 --> 01:24:21,295
So no anonymous posting
for serious game talk.

1781
01:24:21,296 --> 01:24:23,864
That kind of overreach makes me nervous,
too.

1782
01:24:23,865 --> 01:24:25,933
Are we leaning too hard
into big government?

1783
01:24:26,335 --> 01:24:28,236
Isn't that par for the course
when a community is just starting out?

1784
01:24:28,570 --> 01:24:30,004
In the survival phase, yeah.

1785
01:24:30,472 --> 01:24:32,106
We need to stay alive first.

1786
01:24:34,743 --> 01:24:35,610
Dishes duty!

1787
01:24:35,611 --> 01:24:36,978
I'll tackle this counter.

1788
01:24:36,979 --> 01:24:38,312
I'll jump in with you.

1789
01:24:38,313 --> 01:24:39,947
Sounds like a plan. Let's knock it out.

1790
01:24:43,752 --> 01:24:45,886
If names get leaked,

1791
01:24:46,121 --> 01:24:47,221
- you're Karen.
- Deal.

1792
01:24:47,656 --> 01:24:48,956
I'll swap mine.

1793
01:24:50,058 --> 01:24:51,692
And keep it brief.

1794
01:24:51,693 --> 01:24:53,527
The more you type, the guiltier you look.

1795
01:24:55,364 --> 01:24:56,364
What on earth is this?

1796
01:25:00,135 --> 01:25:01,368
Can I take this to my room to read?

1797
01:25:01,770 --> 01:25:02,570
What's the book?

1798
01:25:02,805 --> 01:25:03,671
"Bowling Alone?"

1799
01:25:03,740 --> 01:25:05,607
"The Collapse and Revival
of American Community."

1800
01:25:05,808 --> 01:25:07,008
I know that one.

1801
01:25:07,009 --> 01:25:08,777
- Really? Spill.
- What's it about?

1802
01:25:08,778 --> 01:25:11,880
Bowling leagues used to be the backbone
of local American communities.

1803
01:25:12,748 --> 01:25:15,350
People would hang out, chat, debate,
and bond.

1804
01:25:15,375 --> 01:25:17,909
Then along came television
and televised boxing,

1805
01:25:18,153 --> 01:25:20,621
and league bowling fell off a cliff.

1806
01:25:20,889 --> 01:25:22,556
Covered it back in media theory.

1807
01:25:22,557 --> 01:25:23,891
So the premise is...

1808
01:25:23,892 --> 01:25:25,926
mass media gutting
grassroots community life?

1809
01:25:25,927 --> 01:25:27,862
Exactly.
Bowling leagues are the go-to case study.

1810
01:25:29,098 --> 01:25:30,798
- So interesting.
- Fascinating stuff.

1811
01:25:30,899 --> 01:25:32,967
Everyone turned into couch potatoes...

1812
01:25:32,968 --> 01:25:34,101
glued to boxing matches on the tube.

1813
01:25:34,470 --> 01:25:36,037
Followed by the NFL, obviously...

1814
01:25:36,038 --> 01:25:38,807
Bowling requires showing up in the flesh.

1815
01:25:38,808 --> 01:25:40,809
Staring at a TV screen,

1816
01:25:40,810 --> 01:25:43,311
you're totally isolated,
so collective spaces dry up.

1817
01:25:43,879 --> 01:25:46,347
Television drove the wedge,

1818
01:25:46,348 --> 01:25:48,182
and bowling leagues were just one...

1819
01:25:48,183 --> 01:25:50,151
of the final holdouts wiped out
by the screen.

1820
01:25:50,152 --> 01:25:51,519
That's the gist of it, anyway.

1821
01:25:51,520 --> 01:25:52,420
Makes sense.

1822
01:25:52,888 --> 01:25:53,955
Actually sounds super compelling.

1823
01:25:54,189 --> 01:25:55,022
Right?

1824
01:25:55,190 --> 01:25:56,457
I'll just read right here.

1825
01:25:56,693 --> 01:25:59,059
Mind if I read this passage out loud?

1826
01:25:59,996 --> 01:26:02,330
"John Lambert,
a 64-year-old retired employee..."

1827
01:26:02,331 --> 01:26:04,165
"of the University of Michigan hospital,"

1828
01:26:04,366 --> 01:26:06,801
"had been on the kidney
transplant waitlist..."

1829
01:26:06,802 --> 01:26:08,269
"for three years."

1830
01:26:08,737 --> 01:26:10,571
"Andy Boschma,
a 33-year-old accountant..."

1831
01:26:10,572 --> 01:26:12,540
"who caught wind of Lambert's plight,"

1832
01:26:12,741 --> 01:26:15,443
"stepped up and offered to donate one
of his own kidneys."

1833
01:26:15,677 --> 01:26:17,845
"Lambert and Boschma..."

1834
01:26:17,846 --> 01:26:20,748
"knew each other strictly
through their local bowling league."

1835
01:26:20,816 --> 01:26:23,050
"Boschma was white,
and Lambert was Black."

1836
01:26:23,185 --> 01:26:25,653
"Sharing a bowling lane gave
them a space..."

1837
01:26:25,654 --> 01:26:28,122
"to bridge generational
and racial divides."

1838
01:26:28,490 --> 01:26:29,600
"In small ways like this,"

1839
01:26:29,601 --> 01:26:32,059
"Americans need to reconnect
with one another."

1840
01:26:32,060 --> 01:26:33,994
"That is the central plea of this book."

1841
01:26:36,198 --> 01:26:37,498
Why did I read that out loud, you ask?

1842
01:26:37,733 --> 01:26:39,045
Got me wondering whether,

1843
01:26:39,699 --> 01:26:42,370
in a way, this entire setup is forcing us
onto the same bowling lane.

1844
01:26:43,071 --> 01:26:44,304
Just a passing thought.

1845
01:26:48,844 --> 01:26:51,579
Attention residents,
an announcement from the Community Center.

1846
01:26:52,748 --> 01:26:56,250
Effective immediately,
residents are barred from going outdoors.

1847
01:26:57,386 --> 01:27:01,356
All residents, assemble in the main hall.

1848
01:27:14,369 --> 01:27:15,602
What are they holding us up for?

1849
01:27:15,871 --> 01:27:16,704
No clue.

1850
01:27:17,039 --> 01:27:18,773
Outdoor access is completely cut off.

1851
01:27:18,774 --> 01:27:21,076
They even locked the exits.

1852
01:27:21,077 --> 01:27:22,644
Something big is definitely brewing.

1853
01:27:22,645 --> 01:27:23,945
What, are they declaring martial law now?

1854
01:27:25,147 --> 01:27:26,147
We're completely boxed in.

1855
01:27:26,148 --> 01:27:27,315
Locked down tight.

1856
01:27:28,150 --> 01:27:30,317
Why call another assembly this fast?

1857
01:27:31,887 --> 01:27:34,021
(The announcement begins.)

1858
01:27:34,022 --> 01:27:38,126
The Community Center has
a major announcement.

1859
01:27:38,127 --> 01:27:39,127
- Labeled "Important."
- "Important?"

1860
01:27:39,128 --> 01:27:39,994
Heart's pounding!

1861
01:27:40,295 --> 01:27:42,796
A new resident has arrived...

1862
01:27:43,065 --> 01:27:45,433
outside the Community House.

1863
01:27:45,534 --> 01:27:46,400
Literal goosebumps!

1864
01:27:47,235 --> 01:27:48,802
- You're kidding!
- The incoming resident...

1865
01:27:49,505 --> 01:27:52,240
currently holds zero personal funds.

1866
01:27:54,076 --> 01:27:56,377
Residents,

1867
01:27:56,378 --> 01:27:58,746
alongside the two Lifetime Leader
candidates,

1868
01:27:59,047 --> 01:28:03,151
must now deliberate
on the new resident's resettlement grant.

1869
01:28:03,152 --> 01:28:04,518
- Got it.
- Resettlement grant.

1870
01:28:04,886 --> 01:28:06,153
So she's a real immigrant?

1871
01:28:06,521 --> 01:28:11,625
The two candidates must choose
between two payout options.

1872
01:28:11,626 --> 01:28:12,860
Hold on, here's the kicker.

1873
01:28:13,762 --> 01:28:15,863
The current average personal balance...

1874
01:28:15,864 --> 01:28:19,834
across all residents sits
at roughly 6,000 dollars.

1875
01:28:20,469 --> 01:28:22,336
Option one:

1876
01:28:22,337 --> 01:28:24,572
dock and collect 1,000 dollars...

1877
01:28:24,573 --> 01:28:29,110
from each of the six residents
who voted in favor...

1878
01:28:29,344 --> 01:28:33,347
of the pro-immigrant position
in last night's debate.

1879
01:28:34,249 --> 01:28:36,050
Option two:

1880
01:28:36,251 --> 01:28:40,788
pull the entire 6,000 dollars straight
from the community chest.

1881
01:28:41,256 --> 01:28:42,823
- Out of the community fund?
- The communal pot?

1882
01:28:43,192 --> 01:28:45,092
Once the funding method is locked in,

1883
01:28:45,093 --> 01:28:47,762
the new resident will be cleared to enter.

1884
01:28:48,864 --> 01:28:52,266
This concludes the announcement
from the Community Center.

1885
01:28:53,268 --> 01:28:55,603
Brutal. We could literally leave
her stranded out in the rain for hours.

1886
01:28:55,604 --> 01:28:57,037
We need to sort this out fast.

1887
01:28:57,739 --> 01:28:59,573
Final say rests
with the two candidates, right?

1888
01:29:05,247 --> 01:29:09,084
Deliberate on the resettlement grant.

1889
01:29:09,085 --> 01:29:10,485
If we keep bleeding funds
every time an issue pops up,

1890
01:29:10,486 --> 01:29:11,886
we'll be left with nothing.

1891
01:29:11,887 --> 01:29:13,154
Let's not get carried away...

1892
01:29:13,155 --> 01:29:14,889
spiraling into worst-case scenarios.

1893
01:29:14,890 --> 01:29:16,358
They set the benchmark
at 6,000 dollars, right?

1894
01:29:16,359 --> 01:29:18,058
Plenty of people in this room
don't even have that much.

1895
01:29:18,059 --> 01:29:20,294
Yet a newcomer walks in with 6,000 dollars
handed to them on day one.

1896
01:29:20,295 --> 01:29:21,362
While we've been grinding...

1897
01:29:21,363 --> 01:29:22,563
- and paying taxes from the jump.
- Exactly.

1898
01:29:22,564 --> 01:29:24,932
Basically refugee welfare.

1899
01:29:24,933 --> 01:29:27,201
Just feels like an overly cushy payout.

1900
01:29:27,202 --> 01:29:29,503
If they walk in richer than me on day one,

1901
01:29:29,504 --> 01:29:31,138
I'm voting against it, no question.

1902
01:29:31,139 --> 01:29:33,174
Anything goes as long as it's not pork.

1903
01:29:35,310 --> 01:29:39,947
The two candidates will square off
in a live debate tonight.

1904
01:29:39,948 --> 01:29:42,149
Is it impossible for us
to just coexist in here?

1905
01:29:42,150 --> 01:29:44,151
That's pretty naive.

1906
01:29:44,152 --> 01:29:46,787
You're talking out
of both sides of your mouth.

1907
01:29:47,122 --> 01:29:48,489
It's anyone's match now.

1908
01:29:48,690 --> 01:29:52,292
Two residents have been eliminated.

1909
01:29:52,360 --> 01:29:53,960
- Two of them?
- Say what?

1910
01:29:53,961 --> 01:29:55,963
Enough with the wishful thinking.

1911
01:29:55,964 --> 01:29:57,297
This is a cutthroat survival game.

1912
01:29:57,666 --> 01:30:01,402
Please vacate the House immediately.

1913
01:30:01,403 --> 01:30:03,237
So it's not over till it's over.
